commit 559d4e7122f9a40d4279cc9ce7aa9a82a69333c2
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Sat Jul 4 22:57:11 2026 -0400

    Feed the watchdog's live-byte counter from the bundle codec
    
      The sftp-parallel respawn regression tests uncovered a bug on
      the first run.  The per-worker live counter that the parallel liveness
      classifiers read was bumped by the per-file, range, and batch transfer
      paths but never by the bundle codec.  A worker mid-bundle therefore
      read as "0 bytes moved", and any bundle slower than the born-dead
      window (5s) got its worker SIGTERMed at startup - requeue, respawn,
      same kill - so bundle transfers on throttled (-l) or low-bandwidth
      paths churned forever and never completed.  Found by the new
      sftp-parallel-respawn regress test; reproduces with -j 2 -l 4000 and
      no interference.
    
      Add an sftp_conn_live_account() wrapper (the bundle code sees struct
      sftp_conn as opaque) and bump the counter on the upload side per sent
      WRITE (send_msg paces inside the bandwidth limit, so the counter moves
      from the first second; ack-side counting arrives in cap/2 drain bursts
      that can themselves outlast the detection window on slow paths) and on
      the download side per received hpn-bundle-fetch DATA payload.

