commit 441384ee28d3029ea67e7250104c1dae6a8424a3
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Wed Jul 1 17:47:47 2026 -0400

    ssh: don't loop forever on port fallback when fallback_port == the default port
    
      The HPN try-default-then-fall-back connect loop (ssh.c tryagain) re-entered
      fallback whenever options.port == default_ssh_port(). After falling back,
      options.port becomes fallback_port, so if fallback_port equals the default port
      (FallbackPort set to it, or a services entry that collapses the two) the guard
      stays true and the client re-resolves and reconnects to the same unreachable
      port forever, leaking the previous addrinfo each pass (resolve_host overwrote
      addrs without freeaddrinfo).
    
      Add options.port != options.fallback_port to the guard so it only falls back to
      a genuinely different port (terminating after one fallback), freeaddrinfo the
      old addrs and bail if the re-resolve fails instead of passing NULL to
      ssh_connect, and drop the unreachable trailing exit(255).

commit 182264a9a21b99ce032227f529481e7d4244f98c
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Wed Jul 1 17:08:58 2026 -0400

    Update hpnssh.5 with HPNMemoryLimit option

commit b76b8d86c40396eae34d150effea8cf05eed1b4a
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Wed Jul 1 16:40:50 2026 -0400

    num: guard paramsign against a zero-size/NULL param (latent OOB read)
    
      paramsign() computed srcmsb = param->data_size - 1 (size_t) before checking the
      param type, so a signed OSSL_PARAM with data_size == 0 makes srcmsb wrap to
      SIZE_MAX on little-endian and the signed branch then reads param->data[SIZE_MAX]
      - a wild out-of-bounds read. Not reachable through hpnssh today (the only
      caller, the AES-CTR-MT provider's keylen get, always presents an
      UNSIGNED_INTEGER, which short-circuits to POSITIVE before the deref), but harden
      it: return POSITIVE up front for unsigned, NULL-data, or zero-size params, then
      compute srcmsb (now data_size >= 1, no underflow) and inspect the sign byte.

commit 79f41c4c5a33339e7a4a65177a026cdf3f05f6a3
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Wed Jul 1 16:26:19 2026 -0400

    happyeyeballs: clamp select timeout to >= 0 and stop retrying hard select errors
    
      The RFC 8305 connection-race loop computed the select() timeout (diff) by
      subtracting elapsed time from the stagger delay with no lower
      bound. A scheduling stall past the delay makes diff negative, yielding a
      negative struct timeval that select() rejects with EINVAL; the error path then
      continue'd and, under the default no-timeout config, could re-issue the same bad
      select. Clamp diff to 0 and on a select() error retry only EINTR. A persistent
      hard error now breaks out and returns -1 instead of spinning.

