commit 15e77079a44e3765b23d956046eebd0faa205035
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Wed Jul 1 15:42:34 2026 -0400

    cipher-ctr-mt provider: harden get_params with the OSSL_PARAM API
    
      aes_mt_prov_get_params wrote the version/buildinfo strings by casting p->data
      to (const void **) and assigning directly, assuming every matching OSSL_PARAM
      is an OSSL_PARAM_UTF8_PTR. A caller supplying the param as a UTF8_STRING buffer
      would get an 8-byte pointer written into it, a size-only query (p->data ==
      NULL) would NULL-deref, and it always returned success. Not reachable from
      HPN's own code (it never queries these params), but a provider's get_params is
      callable by OpenSSL's provider machinery, so use the proper OSSL_PARAM_locate +
      OSSL_PARAM_set_utf8_ptr API: it verifies the param type, tolerates a NULL data
      pointer, sets return_size, and returns failure on a type mismatch instead of
      corrupting memory.

commit 0e28ef9c9f0df7134bb60aadf33b0c3e1af9b4d9
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Wed Jul 1 15:14:49 2026 -0400

    Insertion of CC20-MT into alg list can potentially cause problems.
    
    if chacha20-poly1305-mt@hpnssh.org is in a custom Ciphers list
    but not first and serial chacha is absent, the serial name gets
    spliced into the middle of a token. This leads to a mangled proposal
    and spurious NO_CIPHER_ALG_MATCH or a different strong cipher.
    
    Fix it by computing the MT token's real offset in orig with
    strstr (match_list already confirmed it is a token, so strstr
    cannot return NULL), and splice serial CC20 immediately after it.
    MT stays first for the first-match, serial follows as
    its fallback.

commit 48544fd650e7ee9f5e6cbd9cc9c16515ac55a545
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Wed Jul 1 14:00:31 2026 -0400

    sftp verify: detect whole-file size mismatch; stop aborting on malformed server hash replies
    
      Resolves the findings from the Pass-5 review sweep of the verify / server code.
    
      Whole-file verify was size blind. sftp_hpn_verify_repair's
      whole-file mode took the compare length from the local file only
      (stat(local_path)) and both ends hash [0,len), while each server hash handler
      clamps to its own EOF - so only min(local_size, remote_size), the common
      prefix, was ever compared. A dest shorter than the source (a truncated
      download that completed on an early EOF - the client reports
      success at highwater without comparing received bytes to the stat size) or
      longer than the source passed verification as "good". Affects the classic
      single-conn verify and every non-range-split file in parallel mode (range-split
      files tile the full transfer size and were unaffected). Fix: stat the remote,
      require local_size == remote_size, and report a size divergence as a definitive
      mismatch not the localized-corruption case the span repair handles, so it is
      reported, not repaired.
    
      Malformed server hash replies aborted the client (DoS). The whole-file
      remote-hash loop (sftp_hpn_hash_remote_file) had three fatal_fr() on a
      short/garbage EXTENDED_REPLY (parse header / status / hash), which crash the
      whole orchestrator when the worker is one of N parallel streams - the same
      reason the sibling ID-mismatch and wrong-type cases were already made graceful.
      Convert all three to sftp_conn_die + return -1 to fail closed  matching those
      cases and the range path.

commit ba6eead3aade8156a0d1c0dd3ae75a5355cc1153
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Wed Jul 1 13:04:55 2026 -0400

    sftp parallel: cleanup pass - stripe clamp, walker failure recording, range guards, doc + reap hardening
    
      Batch of the remaining low-severity review findings.
    
      sftp-parallel-walk.c: the tiered Lustre small-file threshold is derived
      from the 64-bit stripe size but carried in a u32 (the hpn-file-layout wire
      field and the local lustre setter), so a stripe >= 4 GiB - or an exact
      multiple of 2^32 - truncated to a bogus value (e.g. 0, which disables the
      small-file tier). Add stripe_to_small_threshold() clamping to UINT32_MAX and
      use it at both sites. This is probably never going to happen but it is
      a possibility in some rare lustre configurations.
    
