      Record both, matching every other walker error site.
    
      sftp-client.c: sftp_upload_range/sftp_download_range skipped their
      send/recv loop and returned success for a non-positive range length, which
      would launder a malformed range unit as a completed transfer. submit_*_ranges
      never emits such a range (the striped-split root was already fixed), so this
      is defense-in-depth: reject length <= 0 loudly at both entry points. This
      should never trigger but just in case.
    
      Adding that guard surfaced a genuine latent bug it made the compiler notice:
      sftp_upload_range's two early "goto out" paths (handle/local-file open
      failure) reach the exit debug, which prints bytes_left, before bytes_left is
      assigned - an uninitialized read. Initialize bytes_left = range_length at its
      declaration. Resolved.
    
      sftp-parallel-reporter.c: the reap loop's death classification +
      unproductive-death accounting + last_worker_exit_code were nested under
      if (w->ssh_pid > 0), so a reaped worker with ssh_pid <= 0 would be freed
      unclassified and dropped from the summary. Unreachable today (an in-array
      worker always has ssh_pid > 0), but restructure to gate only the kill/waitpid
      and classify every reaped worker. Correctness fix only.

commit eb285d97eff2846e536bc1020dba150f4476f3fb
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Wed Jul 1 12:36:50 2026 -0400

    sftp parallel respawn: single-probe storm guard + refuse untracked spawn at registry-full
    
      Empty-fleet probe storm. When the fleet is empty (cur_workers == 0) the
      dispatcher launches a SINGLE probe connection rather than num_streams, to
      avoid slamming a possibly-saturated backend. But the branch keyed only on
      (respawn_owed || pending), not on whether a probe was already in flight. The
      probe's respawn thread sleeps ~2s (the FXP_INIT-race cooldown) before
      connecting - longer than the reporter tick (~1s) - so the next tick(s) still
      saw cur_workers == 0 and launched more probes: 2-3 concurrent connections
      instead of one, defeating the single-probe design and inflating
      total_respawns / respawn_epoch_count. Gate the branch on pending_respawns == 0
      (read under the workers_mu the dispatch already holds).
    
      Untracked spawn child at registry-full. spawning_pid_register silently
      did nothing when n_spawning == SFTP_PARALLEL_MAX_WORKERS, but spawn_one_worker
      set registered = 1 regardless and proceeded. The ssh child was then absent
      from the spawning_pids[] registry, so abort/stop's SIGTERM sweep could not
      find it and teardown would block on its connect for a full timeout. Return
      success from spawning_pid_register and, on a full registry (pathological -
      e.g. the B storm above), refuse the spawn: reap the child now and fail,
      leaving respawn_owed in place so the dispatch retries once an in-flight slot
      frees.

commit 48027094e7f83db332113757eaf920bb46b2030b
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Wed Jul 1 12:24:33 2026 -0400

    sftp parallel: complete abort/teardown accounting (tracker finalize + bundle pending)
    
      Two teardown-path accounting fixes in sftp_parallel_stop's cleanup:
    
      parallel_retry_overflow_free (unit.c) freed each parked unit with
      parallel_unit_free alone, which by design never finalizes the shared
      range_tracker. Every other drop site (the abort queue-drain, the worker
      push-fail path) finalizes first. So a range unit parked in the retry-overflow
      list at teardown left its tracker's remaining count above zero - the tracker
      plus its path/src_path/vslots leaked and the incomplete-file finalize was
      skipped. Add the same parallel_unit_tracker_finalize the queue-drain uses; a
      NULL tracker (whole-file / bundle units) is a no-op. Safe: parked overflow
      units never completed, so each still owes exactly one finalize (invariant I1,
      no double-finalize), and this runs after the queue is drained/freed and the
      workers joined, so nothing else references the tracker. Teardown-only, so the
      leaked memory is reclaimed at exit, but the skipped finalize also dropped the
      incomplete-file cleanup.
    
      The abort queue-drain decremented pending by 1 per drained item, but a
      bundle container is a single queue item carrying N members and pending was
      bumped once per member at bundle-add time (parallel_bundle_add). Draining a
      container therefore left pending overstated by n_members - 1. Decrement by
      n_members for a container. Harmless today (terminal stop never reads pending
      again; the drain has a single caller), but keeps the accounting correct for
      any future reuse.

commit b3fc43d782b1fc6a80220b8f4b54d8fca3178f8e
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Wed Jul 1 12:15:58 2026 -0400

    sftp parallel watchdog: fix warmup-gate byte units and guard the time-cap subtraction
    
      Two arithmetic bugs in the slow-start (RAMP) outlier-suppression gate in
      watchdog_sample_throughput:
    
      warmup byte budget was ~8x too small. The gate requires a fresh worker
      to move (peer throughput x path_rtt x RAMP_RTTS) bytes before its EMA is
      judged against the path max. max_ema_kbps is KiB/s (bytes/s / 1024, per the
      raw-kbps computation), but warmup_bytes divided by 8000 - the divisor for a
      kilobits/s rate. Result was ~8x too small, so the gate lifted far too
      early and a worker still in TCP slow-start could be condemned as an outlier
      and killed - the exact respawn-churn loop the gate exists to prevent. Convert
      correctly: KiB/s * 1024 (-> bytes/s) * path_rtt_us / 1e6 (-> per RTT) *
      RAMP_RTTS.
    
      unguarded unsigned subtraction in the time-cap. past_time_cap computed
      now - unit_start guarding only unit_start > 0. The worker stores a fresh
      unit_start_ns after we sample `now`, so unit_start > now wraps the subtraction
      to a huge delta and spuriously trips the cap (lifting the gate early). Add the
      now > unit_start guard, matching the sibling since_unit_start_ns computation in
      watchdog_check_one_worker.

commit 28dab72152a8ec160a63c6d47d9f47f18d9085f1
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Wed Jul 1 11:58:58 2026 -0400

    sftp parallel: make 4 cross-thread stat/tuning scalars _Atomic (fix data races)
    
      Four scalars are read and written from different threads without
      synchronization:
    
      path_rtt_us, born_dead_sec: sftp_parallel_set_path_rtt (main thread) writes
      them AFTER sftp_parallel_start has already created the reporter thread,
      which reads them in the watchdog (watchdog_sample_throughput / born-dead
      gate). Concurrent, unsynchronized.
    
      wedge_terminations, peer_stall_terminations: bumped in classify_worker_death
      on the reporter thread's reap loop, which runs OUTSIDE workers_mu, while
      main reads them in sftp_parallel_get_stats before the reporter is joined
      (the join happens later, in stop). The get_stats read holds workers_mu but
      the writer does not, so the lock does not serialize them.
    
      Fix by qualifying the four declarations _Atomic, matching the house pattern
      already used for the cross-thread control flags (verify_phase_active, stopped,
      ext_interrupt_flag - converted the same way in the 2026-06-28 TSan pass).
      Every existing plain access (=, ++, comparison) becomes atomic automatically;
      no call-site changes. On x86 an atomic load is a plain MOV, so the watchdog
      hot path pays nothing.

commit 3faa1039e24e70e17976f6695ff23cdb16a1fb88
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Wed Jul 1 11:39:25 2026 -0400

    sftp parallel: lock workers_mu around the verify-phase meter loop (fix UAF)
    
      The post-transfer verify meter (reporter thread) summed each worker's
      in-flight hash bytes by iterating p->workers[] and dereferencing w->conn
      without holding workers_mu - unlike its three sibling loops (fleet sample,
      reap, per-worker dump), which all take the lock. During the verify phase the
      worker connections are live (doing the hashing), so a conn that drops
      mid-hash is reaped and a detached respawn thread xreallocarray()s p->workers
      under workers_mu. That realloc/free racing the unlocked meter loop is a
      use-after-free / out-of-bounds read on the workers array or a freed worker
      struct -> crash during verify. This is an unlikely scenario but the
      potential UAF could cause crashes.
    
      Wrap the loop in workers_mu (the body is only a cheap atomic getter, no
      nested locking, and workers_mu is not held at the meter-refresh site).

commit 432df950dfddc783acda8320607644dd133cbdca
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Wed Jul 1 11:18:43 2026 -0400

    sftp parallel: fix respawn UAF; drop dev-only worker stats CSV; working churn notice
    
      Three coupled changes to the respawn / observability path.
    
      Fix a use-after-free of the just-spawned worker. respawn_worker_thread
      dereferenced the worker AFTER spawn_one_worker had already published
      it - inserted into p->workers[] and pthread_create'd its thread. If that
      worker exits immediately (dead conn, abort, born-dead first unit) the
      reporter reap can pthread_join + pthread_mutex_destroy(&w->mu) + free(w)
