      Add a one-time notice keyed on the session-wide death count reaching
      HPN_PATH_CHURN_NOTICE_MULT(2) * num_streams, emitted from
      classify_worker_death alongside the existing per-respawn line.

commit 1f0a40f30b4c38305bec074f59c32839b70870f7
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Wed Jul 1 10:29:27 2026 -0400

    sftp parallel: recompute range count after stripe-aligning range size
    
      This is fixing waht seems to be a pretty rare situation. If the stripe
      size is large (say 128MB) in comparison to the range it's possible
      that the rnage offsets will end up overshooting. compute_range_split rounds
      range_size UP to the destination filesystem's stripe boundary but left the
      range COUNT at its pre-alignment value. When the stripe inflates range_size
      enough that two or more trailing ranges fall at/after EOF, the malformed
      ranges reach the wire: submit_{upload,download}_ranges only drops the single
      final range via its file_size-offset length check, so an interior past-EOF
      range (offset past EOF, length still a full range_size) is submitted,
      driving past-EOF reads plus a negative-length final range, and the transfer
      fails (rc=1). A single trailing overshoot (stripe only modestly larger than
      per-range) is already absorbed by the submit clamp and is unaffected. Only
      triggers on a striped/parallel FS (Lustre/GPFS) where stripe_size > 0 and
      the stripe is roughly >= 2x the per-range size (large-file-tuned stripe, or
      high -M split counts: 200 GiB / -M 2048 / 4 GiB stripe = 50 overshoot
      ranges); ext4/xfs report stripe 0 and take the plain-division path.
    
      Fortunately this is an easy fix.

commit 73f5fcc31747eaf0ab17872bad9a08f73b5ccafd
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Wed Jul 1 09:24:43 2026 -0400

    sftp parallel: don't let saw_perm_denied leak into bundle-member no_retry
    
      The no-retry work set a per-conn saw_perm_denied flag (a PERMISSION_DENIED
      makes a unit no_retry) and cleared it per unit/batch at execute_unit,
      worker_drain_pipeline, and worker_run_batch_pipelined - but not on the
      bundle path. So in worker_finish_bundle a stale flag (from a prior
      per-file EACCES on the same worker) or the bundle's own container-OPEN
      denial made worker_finalize_one_entry mark every bundle member no_retry,
      turning a SERVER_CANT bundle (which should fall back to per-file) into a
      dropped batch. A bundle member has not had its own per-file attempt yet,
      so the flag must not decide its no_retry. Clear saw_perm_denied in
      worker_finish_bundle (the shared upload+download finalize tail) before the
      per-member finalize; POLICY_DENIED still sets no_retry explicitly and
      TRANSPORT_FAILED is guarded by !transient in the finalizer.

