commit 32839fd395cfe716ea2b86a18b02d49bb086137f
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Tue Jun 30 22:34:13 2026 -0400

    sftp-server: revert write-intent-open gating under -P/-p
    
      The prior change made process_open refuse a write-intent open whenever a
      WRITE was not permitted (readonly OR a -P/-p policy), to avoid the empty
      file that a -P write policy otherwise leaves behind. That was an
      unnecessary divergence from upstream: -R (read-only mode) already refuses
      write-intent opens by inspecting the open flags and is the correct
      mechanism for a read-only server, whereas -P is a name-based request
      denylist and using -P write as a read-only tool is a misuse. The change
      also did not fully close -P write (setstat/fsetstat truncation and similar
      remain reachable by name), so it added divergence without finishing the
      job.

