commit e8da213d0c46ca94e590a69fc7669dfd3e619be4
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Tue Jun 30 20:35:40 2026 -0400

    sftp parallel: handle server refusals cleanly; enforce -P/-p on HPN extensions
    
      A -P/-p request-policy denial (or any PERMISSION_DENIED) drove the
      bundle/parallel path into a respawn storm, endless per-file retries,
      worker kills, and empty-file litter, and could grind through every file
      before failing. HPN's own extensions also slipped past -P/-p entirely.
      This reworks refusal handling end to end:
    
      Bundle drains treat a non-OK WRITE/READ status as an authoritative
      server refusal, not a transport death: drain in sync, keep the
      connection, classify SERVER_CANT (no respawn storm). The download
      drain resyncs before CLOSE so a refused read no longer desyncs into a
      worker-killing protocol violation.
    
      A PERMISSION_DENIED marks the unit no_retry on every upload/download
      path (sticky per-conn flag set at the status reader and the inline ack
      sites), so a refusal fails once instead of burning MAX_RETRIES.
    
      process_open refuses a write-intent open whenever writes are not
      permitted (readonly or a -P/-p policy), so a write-denied policy no
      longer leaves empty files behind. stock OpenSSH gates this on readonly
      only, so a -P write / -p (no-write) policy still allowed the create and
      left an empty file the client could never write; this extends the same
      flag check to the request policy. readonly behavior is unchanged.
    
      Policy-denied fast-abort: the server tags a request-policy
      PERMISSION_DENIED, the client detects the tag and aborts the whole
      transfer instead of re-denying every file. Thsi is important because with
      many many files in a batch or glob it could take a very long time for
      each file to be individually denied for what is a site wide policy issue.
    
      hpn-check-file, sftp-hash-range, hpn-file-layout, and hpn-fs-info are
      mapped to their equivalent op class (read/write/statvfs) so -P/-p
      covers them; the bundle data handlers stay advertised so the fast-abort
      still fires. Man page documents the mapping and what each costs when
      denied.

