commit 05b46086e5e6d46fd74f27876b0c31422598879f
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Tue Jun 30 14:27:56 2026 -0400

    bundle: reject records larger than the bundle maximum before allocating
    
      The bundle codec parser passed each entry's declared size (a client-
      controlled u64) straight to the consumers, which malloc((size_t)size)
      and posix_fallocate() up front, before any data arrives. A malicious
      client (or server, on download) could declare an implausibly large size
      to make the peer reserve that much RAM/disk with no data sent -- an
      amplification regular SFTP has no equivalent for (SFTP only consumes
      resources as bytes actually arrive). On 64-bit the only prior guard
      (size > SIZE_MAX) compiled out, so the declared size was unbounded.
    
      The bundle path only carries small whole files (a file is bundle-eligible
      only below HPNBundleSize/4, and HPNBundleSize is itself capped at
      HPN_BUNDLE_SIZE_MAX), so a record larger than a whole bundle's maximum is
      never legitimate. Reject size > HPN_BUNDLE_SIZE_MAX (256 MiB) at the
      parser before the consumers allocate -- a single choke point covering all
      four bundle paths, which also subsumes the old ILP32 size_t-truncation
      guard (HPN_BUNDLE_SIZE_MAX < SIZE_MAX on every supported platform).

commit dea04771d9233514a7ad3d5cd1925bba5b075edb
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Tue Jun 30 13:05:35 2026 -0400

    bundle: bound the pool-path data_cb memcpy against the entry size
    
      The writer-pool upload and download data callbacks memcpy received bytes into a
      per-entry buffer sized to the tar-method header's declared size, trusting the
      parser to never deliver more than that. Add a local bound (written as a
      subtraction so the check itself can't overflow) so a parser regression
      cannot overflow the buffer; on violation the entry is aborted. The tar-method
      parser already clamps delivered data to the declared size, so this is
      defense-in-depth with no behavior change.

commit a650705bd9d67494e75aac55285c9d1ac4286d89
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Tue Jun 30 12:52:10 2026 -0400

    sftp-server: don't refuse symlinks in the HPN verify probes
    
      process_hpn_hash_range and process_hpn_check_file opened the verify
      target with O_RDONLY|O_NOFOLLOW, but the data path and regular SFTP
      follow symlinks. So a transfer to/from a symlinked path succeeded, yet
      the post-transfer verify then ELOOP'd on the same path and reported a
      spurious failure (potentially triggering verify-repair). O_NOFOLLOW
      added no security here -- the client supplies the path and could name
      the symlink target directly -- so drop it from both probes to match the
      data path

