commit 2021542839767a0db3d41957b49a9a753c83cd92
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Tue Jun 30 11:47:52 2026 -0400

    scp: apply the per-user worker cap per connection, not once globally
    
      scp_parallel_launch() clamped the requested stream count to the server's
      advertised hpn-max-workers cap behind a function-static "applied once"
      flag, and by mutating the global parallel_num_streams. But downloads
      relaunch the orchestrator per source argument (each may be a different
      host), so only the first host's cap was ever applied -- later hosts
      inherited the first host's clamped count and never consulted their own.
    
      Compute the effective count into a per-call local (eff_streams) from the
      immutable parallel_num_streams request against this connection's cap, and
      drop the now-redundant parallel_num_streams=1 in the orchestrator-start
      failure path (routing is on parallel_orch). Each connection now caps
      against the original -j request -- which also sets up the 3rd-party /
      crossload case where every leg is its own server.

