commit 3c5a6ad0d083f48abbf93f8ef156e633b0cf6344
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Mon Jun 29 18:03:12 2026 -0400

    Fixes from the MT-cipher review pass:
    
      aes_mt_do_cipher (OpenSSL 3 provider) never set *destlen, violating the
      OSSL cipher update/cipher contract. Set it to the processed length.
      Benign on the current EVP_Cipher path (callers check only < 0) but
      fragile.
    
      The OpenSSL 3 provider advertised no ivlen or mode params, so
      EVP_CIPHER_CTX_iv_length() reported 0 for the MT cipher. Advertise
      ivlen (16) and mode (CTR) in get_params and the gettable table so the
      cipher describes itself correctly. No live consumer on the MT data
      path; the privsep handoff (cipher_get_keyiv) runs on the non-MT cipher.
    
      The AES-CTR worker loops (both the OSSL 3 provider and the
      OSSL 1.x path) ignored the return values of EVP_CIPHER_CTX_new and
      the EVP_Encrypt* calls: an OOM or init failure would NULL-deref or
      silently produce a garbage keystream. Check them and fatal cleanly,
      matching the chachapoly path.
    
      chachapoly_crypt_mt gated the write of decrypted plaintext on
      "r != SSH_ERR_MAC_INVALID". Equivalent today (every other error path
      returns early), but fragile: a future fall-through error would emit
      unauthenticated plaintext. Replace with an explicit mac_ok flag,
      initialized false for decrypt so any path that does not verify the tag
      fails safe.

commit 2cca8b068d525cf33ee5180a71b472e8ed1acd97
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Mon Jun 29 16:33:12 2026 -0400

    Store AES-CTR-MT keylen consistently: bits internally, bytes at the provider boundary
    
      The provider's get/set_ctx_params handled aes_mt_ctx_st->keylen in bytes
      (OpenSSL's OSSL_CIPHER_PARAM_KEYLEN convention) while start_threads and
      the thread keyschedule selector use it in bits (256/192/128). It only
      worked by accident: when start_threads derives the field it is still
      zero, so OpenSSL falls back to the cipher's fixed key length. Now that
      the get/set_ctx_params context-cast bug is fixed, a same-ctx re-key could
      read the bits value back as bytes and multiply by 8, fataling with
      "Invalid key length 2048".
    
      Convert at the param boundary: get_ctx_params returns keylen/8 (bits to
      bytes), set_ctx_params stores keyl*8 (bytes to bits). The field stays
      bits internally and the bits->bytes->bits round-trip is idempotent. Add
      cross-referencing comments at the boundary and at the bits-derivation
      site, and clarify the newctx comment to describe the app_data/keylen
      mechanism.

commit 611d58df7a52f3ca9b9116adfdabf2b99739592a
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Mon Jun 29 16:17:41 2026 -0400

    Fix OpenSSL 3 provider crash in AES-CTR-MT get/set_ctx_params
    
      aes_mt_get_ctx_params() and aes_mt_set_ctx_params() cast the incoming
      void *vctx directly to struct aes_mt_ctx_st *. But newctx returns the
      EVP_CIPHER_CTX (with the real aes_mt_ctx_st attached as app_data), so
      the cast aimed a ~16.5MB struct at the small EVP_CIPHER_CTX. Reading
      ctx->ongoing near the end of the struct was an out-of-bounds read that
      crashed on some heap layouts (seen on musl/Alpine) and, on layouts that
      did not crash, the ctx->keylen write scribbled into the EVP_CIPHER_CTX
      internals. This is the default path for all OpenSSL 3 builds.
    
      Make both functions resolve the context via EVP_CIPHER_CTX_get_app_data()
      with a NULL guard, matching freectx/start_threads/do_cipher. Also zero
      the context in the three newctx variants so no field is read
      uninitialized, and drop the unused 'ongoing' field and its error code.

commit 6a80e2799cdf48dae96f9ea88311eefe94b4b4a1
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Mon Jun 29 12:45:07 2026 -0400

    chacha20-poly1305-mt: fix keystream over-read on oversized packets
    
      The MT cipher precomputes a fixed KEYSTREAMLEN (SSH_IOBUFSZ + 128 = 32896)
      bytes of ChaCha20 keystream per sequence number and XORs the payload
      against it with fastXOR2. But the payload length is bounded by the
      peer-advertised channel maxpacket (taken unclamped) and the pre-auth
      packet_max_size, both of which can exceed 32896 - so a larger packet read
      past mainStream into adjacent heap and XORed it into the wire ciphertext.
    
      The single-threaded chachapoly has no fixed keystream buffer (it generates
      keystream inline for any length). Match that for the overflow: when the
      payload exceeds KEYSTREAMLEN, encrypt the whole payload directly through a
      crypt-thread-owned ChaCha20 context, with the same init as
      generate_keystream's main stream (counter block 1, seqnr nonce) so OpenSSL
      advances the counter across every block. The common (<= KEYSTREAMLEN) path
      is unchanged.
    
      We handle oversized packets rather than reject them because legitimate
      ones exist: pre-auth userauth packets are bounded only by the 256 KiB
      transport limit, and large GSSAPI/Kerberos tokens (the Active Directory
      MaxTokenSize case) and large certificate chains routinely exceed 32 KiB.
      Rejecting would break those logins, but only over chacha20-poly1305-mt -
      a confusing cipher-specific auth failure the single-threaded cipher does
      not have. See the comment at the crypt site for details.
    
      Impact is low - the over-read bytes are the connection's own keystream
      under a key both endpoints already share, and the default 32 KiB maxpacket
      keeps payloads under the buffer - but it is a heap over-read and a
      crash/corruption risk against any peer advertising a larger maxpacket.

