commit 7c3b115d931cae97bf67f29e8063add84a522a80
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Sun Jun 28 16:54:01 2026 -0400

    cipher-ctr-mt: compute thread/queue counts once to fix TSan data race
    
      get_thread_count() rewrote the globals cipher_threads and numkq on every
      newkeys/rekey (via aes_mt_newctx_*), racing the running cipher worker
      threads that read numkq in thread_loop. TSan flagged this 150 times
      across the regression suite's cipher/rekey tests.
    
      The values are deterministic ($SSH_CIPHER_THREADS, fixed for the life of
      the process), so recomputing them is always a no-op after the first call.
      Gate the body behind a one-time guard so numkq/cipher_threads are written
      exactly once, before any cipher thread exists; later newkeys reuse the
      cached values. get_thread_count() is only ever called from the main
      thread, so a plain static guard needs no lock, and this keeps an atomic
      out of the cipher's inner loop.

commit a72f7a7e21ad6fdb9697105e3b507771ef8016c0
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Sun Jun 28 16:39:16 2026 -0400

    sftp parallel: atomic orchestrator flags to fix TSan data races
    
      ThreadSanitizer found 5 data races in the parallel SFTP client: the main
      thread writes several orchestrator control flags while the worker and
      reporter threads read them without synchronization. None corrupted data,
      but they are data races (UB) and a couple had real, if narrow, windows
      (e.g. a worker reading a stale per-command preserve flag).
    
      Qualify the shared flags _Atomic at their declarations so every access
      becomes atomic. This also closes the latent sibling reads TSan did not
      happen to trigger.  No logic or access-site changes.

commit f50190d8bb42003ef47a2b0ea2a91f87cb62e2f0
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Sun Jun 28 14:09:23 2026 -0400

    ftp-parallel-unit: guard NULL unit once in parallel_unit_submit
    
      parallel_unit_submit() checked `u != NULL` in two branches but then
      dereferenced `u` unconditionally - an inconsistent "is it nullable?" that
      cppcheck flagged. u is contractually non-NULL (callers build units via
      make_unit()/xcalloc, which fatal on OOM), so add one early
      `if (u == NULL) return -1;` guard and drop the two now-redundant checks.
      No behaviour change for real callers; defensive and cppcheck-clean.

