commit 3f385c9f31ae230e5e12932f0165eca12feeca38
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Sat Jun 27 22:42:17 2026 -0400

    HPNMaxConcurrentWorkers: deliver the cap via argv, not an env var
    
      Commit 133653814 ("HPNMaxConcurrentWorkers: wire up as an advisory negotiated
      worker cap") made the server advertise the operator's per-user parallel-worker
      cap to the client as the SFTP extension hpn-max-workers@hpnssh.org and had the
      client orchestrator clamp -j to it. To get the resolved cap to the
      hpnsftp-server child, that commit passed it through the
      HPN_MAX_CONCURRENT_WORKERS environment variable.
    
      Remove the env var that we were using to test this.
    
      Hand the cap to the server on its command line instead: session_subsystem_req
      appends "-W <cap>" to the external sftp subsystem exec, and sftp-server reads it
      via getopt into hpn_max_concurrent_workers (replacing the getenv). argv is not
      in the user-reachable environment namespace, so the cap can no longer be
      overridden. The HPN_MAX_CONCURRENT_WORKERS env export is removed
      (HPN_USE_BUNDLE / HPN_WRITER_POOL are left as-is).

commit 133653814e9d548f25ea0d6c998d9a0890fc668c
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Sat Jun 27 21:11:19 2026 -0400

    HPNMaxConcurrentWorkers: wire up as an advisory negotiated worker cap
    
      HPNMaxConcurrentWorkers parsed and was even passed to the sftp-server child
      (HPN_MAX_CONCURRENT_WORKERS env), but nothing ever read it: no enforcement,
      no clamp, dead option. The man page also described a counter-file enforcement
      model that was never built. Make it functional as an advisory, negotiated cap.
    
      Mechanism:
    
      Server: the sftp-server advertises hpn-max-workers@hpnssh.org in its
      SSH_FXP_VERSION reply, value = the resolved HPNMaxConcurrentWorkers
    
      Client: sftp_init parses it into conn->hpn_max_workers_cap (-1 = absent,
      0 = no cap, N>0 = cap); sftp_hpn_max_workers_cap() exposes it.
    
      Orchestrator (sftp.c/scp.c): clamp -j before spawning workers:
         cap < 0  (stock / non-HPN server, no advertisement) -> min(req, 8)
         cap == 0 (HPN server, no admin cap)                 -> honor (<= 24)
         cap > 0  (HPN policy)                               -> min(req, cap)
      and tell the user when the request is reduced. The hard
      SFTP_PARALLEL_MAX_WORKERS ceiling (24) is unchanged.
    
      This is advisory: it constrains cooperating HPN clients, not a hostile or
      non-HPN one. Hard limits remain the server's/OS's job (MaxStartups,
      PerSourceMaxStartups, PAM, cgroups), which the rewritten man-page entry now
      says honestly.

commit 71810acb104532070d3ce3c470ea90de44c6f95c
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Sat Jun 27 19:02:50 2026 -0400

    Propagate connection flags to parallel-streams workers
    
      Parallel workers are built from a fresh argv out of
      sftp_parallel_config, not from the main connection's arglist, so any flag
      captured only into that arglist never reached the workers. The main/control
      connection honored these flags but every worker ignored them:
    
        -4 / -6  address family   (workers could resolve to the other family)
        -C       compression
        -c       cipher
        -J       ProxyJump         (workers fail outright if the host is only
                                    reachable through the jump)
        -s       remote subsystem / sftp-server path (sftp only)
    
      Each connection-shaping flag has an exact -o equivalent, so push it onto the
      forwarded -o list the workers already consume: -4/-6 -> AddressFamily,
      -C -> Compression, -c -> Ciphers, -J -> ProxyJump. The remote subsystem/path
      (-s) gets a dedicated pcfg field; the worker argv now mirrors the main
      connection's logic (a spec containing '/' runs as a server command with no
      -s, otherwise it is a -s subsystem request defaulting to "sftp").
    
      -A (agent forwarding) is deliberately not forwarded to data-mover workers,
      and -q is left off so worker connection errors stay visible.

