commit 8a4fe01e1bdffbe65f3e4c2bd08d36318f3e8457
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Thu Jun 25 15:11:13 2026 -0400

    sftp: byte-budget backpressure for the bundle writer pool
    
      The prior work is effective but we can hit some excessive memory usage
      in that the entire bundle was stored in memory. Which could lead to
      situations where the transfer process could consum more than 1 GB of
      memory. We clamping the number of bytes allocated to the writer pool
      we essentially push each pool to work on one bundle at a time so the
      memory max is equal to the bundle size.
    
      Gate pool enqueue on buffered bytes, not just queued-job count, so the
      extract pool's memory is bounded regardless of file-size mix. New
      HPN_BUNDLE_WRITER_BUDGET; default is the client's bundle size, now sent
      as a u64 on hpn-bundle-open. The old QUEUE_DEPTH knob becomes the count
      backstop. Adds a lone-file-over-budget deadlock guard and a wake on byte
      release.

