commit e60cd4dac28c8ef3a2cf7bdb8288c246276b401c
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Wed Jun 24 16:39:49 2026 -0400

    sftp: optional parallel writer pool for bundle extract (default off)
    
      The server unpacks a bundle serially - one open/create/close per file,
      which on Lustre is an MDS round-trip each and the small-file ceiling.
      With HPN_BUNDLE_WRITER_THREADS > 1 the parser callbacks buffer each
      complete file and hand it to a bounded pool of writer threads that do
      the open/write/close, so the per-file metadata round-trips overlap;
      the close handler joins the pool before replying.  Default 1 = the
      unchanged serial path.  Contained to sftp-hpn-bundle-server.c.
    
      will be testing on br008 (our source for lustre access).

commit ba7575dbecb2028156da0c32e3597e8a14a96f2e
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Wed Jun 24 15:41:18 2026 -0400

    sftp: minimal bundle record format; fix preserve on the bundle path
    
      We originally did the bundling as a pretty strict implementation of
      tar. While this was effective it was also a lot of code overhead and
      the framing added bytes we really didn't need.  So replace the bundle
      codec's tar/USTAR framing with a length-prefixed binary record -
      type, big-endian mode/mtime/size/path_len, path, then data, with no
      padding; a lone type=0 byte ends the stream. We wanted the concept of tar
      but not the overhead of a protocol compliant implmentation.
    
      Also fix permission preservation:
      - Force the exact mode with fchmod on both extract sides (upload server,
        download client): open(O_CREAT, perm) is subject to umask and ignored
        on a pre-existing file, so preserved modes were silently masked (mtime
        was already exact via futimens).
      - Route a per-command put/get -p to the long-lived parallel orchestrator
        (sftp_parallel_set_preserve); it previously took preserve only from
        the program-level -p, so the parallel/bundle path ignored per-command
        -p.

