commit 526032ba23f8d6cbe5f088ed9b65ee618ab5169f
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Tue Jun 23 18:28:11 2026 -0400

     Verify memory gate: charge at submit, not at park
    
      The gate's trigger checked parked_bytes at submit time, but that counter
      only changed when a worker finished a file and parked it. On upload those
      events interleave, so the check happened to catch the budget crossing and
      waves fired. On download they don't: the walker submits every unit before
      any file is fetched and parked, so parked_bytes was ~0 at every submit
      check and the wave never fired - the whole transfer parked at the end,
      unbounded. The trigger watched a lagging signal the submitter couldn't see.
    
      Charge the parked-verify footprint at SUBMIT instead - a leading signal.
      parallel_verify_item_bytes_estimate() computes a file's item size up front
      (same prefix factoring as the real park, so the estimate equals the actual
      size), and sftp_parallel_submit_upload/_download add it before the wave
      check; the park-side increments are removed. Now the submitter sees the
      memory coming and the wave fires in both directions, and submit blocking
      during the wave is the backpressure that paces submission with verify.
    
      This also tightens the bound: outstanding is capped at the budget before
      the wave runs, so the wave's quiesce only ever drains one budget's worth -
      peak = budget + one file, vs the old budget + an unbounded in-flight set.

commit 25a2d087b81b2877b908ca1058c09307d13aedd7
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Tue Jun 23 17:10:09 2026 -0400

    Bundle codec: variable-length path, drop the USTAR 100/255 limit
    
      The bundle tar split the path into USTAR compliance, so a
      filename component over 100 chars (or a path over 255) could not be
      represented - the writer rejected such files and the bundle silently
      skipped them. Legal names (NAME_MAX 255) were undeliverable via a bundle.
    
      The bundle stream is HPN client <-> HPN server only, never read by an
      external tar, so we are free to extend it. Store the path as one
      contiguous variable-length field: its length in the (unused, no-symlink)
      linkname slot, the bytes running from a fixed block-0 offset straight
      into extra 512-byte blocks when long. Read/write is a single memcpy;
      tar_split_path and the name/prefix juggling are gone; cap is now
      PATH_MAX. Short paths stay one block; longer ones just lengthen the
      header. Parser reads block 0, learns the length, collects any spill,
      then handles the entry.

commit 70ec8993549a4ab65941f1dbb9e26d434d06d36b
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Tue Jun 23 15:49:27 2026 -0400

    Cap download bundle fetch request at SFTP_MAX_MSG_LENGTH
    
      The hpn-bundle-fetch download request lists every member's remote path
      in one SFTP message, but the batch was only bounded by data bytes
      (bundle_target_bytes) and file count (BUNDLE_BATCH_MAX_FILES = 8192) -
      neither of which bounds the path list. For small files the count cap
      binds first, so a download bundle of more than ~1900 files (paths
      totalling >256 KiB) overflowed SFTP_MAX_MSG_LENGTH and fatally aborted
      the transfer ("Outbound message too long"). A single getv of a
      directory with that many small files failed outright. Upload is immune:
      it streams paths inside the tar, never in one request.
    
      Bound the download bundle by request size as well. Add
      BUNDLE_DL_FETCH_REQ_MAX (SFTP_MAX_MSG_LENGTH - PATH_MAX - 1024, leaving
      room for one path's overshoot plus the request header) and track the
      accumulated request bytes (4-byte cstring length + remote path per
      member) in both producers: the worker batch accumulator (stops adding
      cleanly, leaving the next unit queued) and parallel_bundle_add (flushes
      the container). Gated SFTP_OP_DOWNLOAD-only, so upload bundling is
      unchanged. It's a byte cap, so it adapts to path length, and one path
      can't exceed PATH_MAX, so there is no orphan case.
    
      A large directory now fans out into multiple sub-limit fetches instead
      of failing. Validated: 30000-file recursive download -> 15 chunked
      fetches (max n=2057), zero "message too long", all byte-identical;
      10000-file upload unaffected, byte-identical.

commit 0167556c3f070072589deceaa2031b30c50796e7
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Tue Jun 23 15:07:46 2026 -0400

    Parked-verify memory gate (Work In Progress)
    
      Bound the peak memory of the post-transfer verify "parked" set, which
      otherwise grows without limit as file count grows. A fleet-wide byte
      counter tracks parked-path memory (whole-file items + range trackers +
      prefix pool); when it crosses a budget the main-thread submitter runs a
      verify WAVE: quiesce in-flight transfers, drain the whole parked set
      through the verify phase, free it, and reset the prefix pool. Hard pause,
      full drain. Budget default 64 MiB, ENV-VAR HPN_VERIFY_PARK_BUDGET_MB.
      A second trigger fires when the prefix pool nears its INT16_MAX cap.
    
      Counter increments at the three park sites and resets to 0 in
      parallel_verify_phase_submit (frees happen only inside the drain, so no
      per-free decrement). Trigger is parallel_verify_maybe_wave(), called at
      the tail of sftp_parallel_submit_upload/_download (main-thread only;
      workers never call those). Active only when verify is enabled.
    
      Need to address an issue that came up with SFTP_MAX_MSG_LENGTH blocking
      the number fo small files we can put into a bundle. this gets resolved
      with the next commit and then we come back to this to test.

commit 0a17b018e444407a42890be8085ee13410a035c6
