Date:   Tue Jun 23 13:32:58 2026 -0400

    Resolve potential use after free introduced by verify work.

commit 2bcfc6f24cab2cbeea8fec08413949278dbc1652
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Tue Jun 23 13:06:06 2026 -0400

    Seven files, all the verify path-factoring/memory work from this session:
    
      Per-side prefix factoring + single-allocation parked verify item
    
      Rework whole-file verify path factoring from a (local,remote) base
      pair into a per-side directory-prefix pool, so it factors globs (which
      bypass the walker) and works identically on upload and download. Each
      path side matches the longest registered prefix independently; the
      always-full remote side factors, a relative local side stays as-is.
      The walker registers both command roots; the glob/direct dispatch in
      process_put/process_get registers each file's dirs via the new public
      sftp_parallel_register_verify_dir().
    
      Shrink the parked item to one flexible-array allocation: header plus
      "local_rel\0remote_rel\0" in a single malloc/free instead of a struct
      plus two xstrdups. Prefix indices are int16_t (pool capped at
      INT16_MAX, graceful full-path fallback past it); all size arithmetic
      lives in one constructor, copies bounded by the measured lengths.
      Floor drops from ~120 to ~40 bytes per parked file. This is the
      minimum size - the actuul per element size is this *plus* the
      actual path data.
    
      This was a necessary first step in order to minimize the number of time
      we need to pause a transfer in order to conduct a verification/integrity
      check. that's the next step in this.

commit 221f717bf5f84ffb324cd70ffa1b5998533a17a8
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Tue Jun 23 11:18:51 2026 -0400

    Factor whole-file verify paths against a per-command base pool
    
      A whole-file verify item held two full paths per file - the dominant
      verify-phase memory term in a deep recursive transfer. This meant the
      full paths for every file were stored in memory which could cause
      significant memory usage over time (about .5kB per file). Reducing
      the memory footprint is important.
    
      Instead, we can store the paths relative to a small deduped base pool
      instead: each recursive command registers its (local_root, remote_root)
      once at the walk entry, and each item keeps base_index + a relative suffix
      (remote_rel NULL = equals local_rel, the mirror case). The long common
      prefix then lives once, not in 2N paths; single files / disparate globs
      fall back to full paths (base_index -1). We need to review the glob situation
      though.
    
      The park recovers the base by longest-prefix-matching the file's path, so
      it is correct regardless of which command is "current" when a file parks
      - which keeps it right under batch-mode defer, where a whole script's
      files accumulate before the verify phase runs. Registration gates on
      p->cfg.verify_transfer (the flag the park checks), not the upload_dir /
      download_dir verify arg, which is 0 even for putv.

