commit fcfc7d2be67f8acf6e9bf15fd80d45d4dd1c726d
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Mon Jun 22 17:56:47 2026 -0400

    Unify post-transfer repair into one inline splice engine
    
      Collapse the two repair paths into one. A new chunked_reconcile_span()
      is the single splice engine - the resume helpers and per-range
      auto-repair all use it - and sftp_hpn_verify_repair() is range-scoped,
      verifying through one path (sftp_hpn_verify_chunk). The orchestrator
      now repairs inline in each verify unit, so the separate #6 repair phase
      is deleted: parallel_repair_phase_run, the SFTP_OP_REPAIR op + handler,
      parallel_repair_park, repair_item, repair_pending, and the dead
      verify_job range_failed/range_dest_hash.
    
      Range-split repair now splices only the bad 64 MiB chunks instead of
      re-sending the whole transfer range (2 GiB observed), matching the
      whole-file path. verify_chunk reads O_DIRECT only for the written dest
      and buffered for an upload source, so verify stays as cheap as before.
      Also removes the now-dead whole-file verify_transfer front-end and the
      src_take bridge.
    
      Client-side only, no wire change. Net -569 lines. Validated against a
      19.0 server: 24/24 verify/repair matrix, a 10 GiB range-split
      corruption repaired inline (64 MiB spliced, byte-exact), resume
      unchanged.

commit b96c2d4900c171bdfd4a2779249cdb4f8243fa28
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Mon Jun 22 16:42:43 2026 -0400

    Add interrupt check to the repair loop and de-jargon repair messages
    
      Two follow-ups to the shared verify+repair core. First,
      the core's repair loop now polls the interrupted SIGINT flag
      between attempts, so a converging or capping repair of a large
      file bails promptly on Ctrl-C instead of grinding through
      every remaining attempt. The flag is defined in both sftp.c
      and scp.c and is set in both the classic and parallel contexts;
      on interrupt the file is left as the last attempt wrote it and
      recorded as a verify failure.
    
      Second, normalize the user-facing repair messages to be file-centric
      and drop "range", which means nothing to users.

