commit 2341784602b1c15aa10e1e59b2709b9e10907394
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Sun Jun 21 16:41:18 2026 -0400

    sftp: O_DIRECT read-back in the server's range-hash verify
    
      The server's sftp-hash-range handler used buffered reads, so an upload
      verify hashed the page cache - the copy still warm from the just-finished
      write - rather than what actually landed on disk. Downloads already read
      back via O_DIRECT client-side; uploads now match. This breaks the
      integrity guarantee.
    
      Factor the fdatasync + posix_fadvise(DONTNEED) + O_DIRECT setup into a
      shared sftp_hpn_fd_set_ondisk(), used by both the client read-back and the
      server, and read through a 4 MiB page-aligned buffer with a buffered
      fallback on EINVAL.

commit 19a8c764fe4e2c588d7a607ed00a54240e6a1339
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Sun Jun 21 12:26:43 2026 -0400

    sftp: range-granular parallel verify of large files
    
      Spread a large file's post-transfer verify across the worker pool instead
      of one worker hashing the whole file: each transfer range becomes its own
      verify unit, fanned over the -j workers, so a single huge file is checked
      as parallel as it was transferred.
    
      Both directions use one mechanism (sftp_hpn_verify_chunk): hash the local
      range vs the server's sftp-hash-range of the remote range and compare.
      Uploads re-use the source hash teed during the transfer (no re-read);
      downloads, and any untee'd range, O_DIRECT read the range back via the new
      sftp_hpn_hash_range_ondisk (the whole-file hasher now wraps it).
    
      Re-uses the existing transfer ranges (vslots) as the verify chunks - no
      separate chunk size, and big files get full -j parallelism for free.
      range_tracker_new now allocates vslots for downloads too (it only did so
      for uploads, so download verify had silently fallen back to whole-file);
      the download submit fills their offsets. A per-file verify_job refcounts
      the chunks and the last one records any failure and frees it.
    
      Small files, and servers without sftp-hash-range, keep the one-unit
      whole-file verify. Mismatches now name the exact failing byte range.

