commit 8090b37f1bdc8e60ad3573354d6af7c441c63e5a
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Fri Jun 19 23:00:16 2026 -0400

    sftp-parallel: fix two -j1 liveness deadlocks in transfer recovery
    
      Both are degenerate at -j1, where the single worker is the only consumer
      of the work queue. Found while investigating a j1 small-file put that
      wedged at 0% CPU partway through. We do not expect -j1 to ever be in
      production but issues here can have issues at higher values of j.
    
      1) Worker re-queue self-deadlock (the observed hang, caught via gdb).
         Workers re-queue units onto p->q with the BLOCKING sftp_workqueue_push.
         When a bundle fails it re-queues every member (~3856 units for a 32 MiB
         bundle of 8 KB files); with the walker's backlog already queued, the
         push hits a full ring and blocks. At -j1 that blocked worker is the
         only consumer, so nothing drains the queue and it hangs forever (also
         reachable at higher -j under correlated bundle failures).
    
         Worker re-queues are now non-blocking. All five worker push sites
         (bundle retry, process-result retry/yield, yield-handoff defer,
         cap-gate, endgame leftover) go through parallel_worker_requeue(): try
         the queue, and on a full queue park the unit on a per-transfer overflow
         list instead of blocking, so the worker stays a live consumer. The
         reporter drains the overflow back into p->q as space frees. Parked
         units stay counted in p->pending, so sftp_parallel_wait cannot
         false-complete, and are freed at teardown. Adds sftp_workqueue_trypush
         and sftp_workqueue_trypush_front.
    
      2) Stalled-respawn watchdog (a latent deadlock in the same path).
         A respawn whose ssh handshake stalls after the TCP connect blocks
         spawn_one_worker in sftp_init forever: ConnectTimeout bounds only the
         connect, not kex/auth. That pins pending_respawns > 0, and the dead-end
         abort net is gated on pending_respawns == 0, so it never fires and the
         orchestrator waits forever (fatal at -j1, no surviving worker).
    
         The reporter now stamps each in-flight spawn (spawning_since[]) and
         SIGTERMs any stalled past HPN_RESPAWN_STALL_SEC (30s); the child's exit
         fails sftp_init, deregisters the pid, and lets the normal reap/retry/
         abort recovery proceed.
    
      3) Turns out both of these only came up wehn talking to a non-parallel
         aware server. The client would send a bundle and have it immediately
         rejected causing the bundle to be reloaded on the same worker causing the
         wedge. This was an annoying oversight *but* proves that it's still
         cross compatible.

commit 5cefc985292e324e56639f8c6f4dbe7cfb9fbbe0
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Fri Jun 19 20:09:20 2026 -0400

      sftp: make bundles transfer-only and verify every parallel path post-transfer
    
      The HPNVerifyTransfer post-transfer phase now covers every parallel
      transfer path, and verify is removed from the bundle wire protocol.
    
      Bundle is transfer-only.  Stripped the in-protocol verify on both ends:
      the client read-back/compare and the source-hash tee, the server CLOSE
      read-back, the per-file hash exchange in the CLOSE reply, the
      HPN_BUNDLE_FLAG_VERIFY flag, and the SFTP_HPN_BUNDLE_REPLY_SENT sentinel.
      The bundle CLOSE is now a plain STATUS in both directions. Note:
      The entire bundle routine may end up being removed in favor of
      more straightforward pipe like process.
    
      Every batched/bundled member now parks for the post-transfer verify
      phase in the shared worker_finalize_one_entry.  This closes a real gap:
      the non-bundle pipelined upload batch (worker_run_batch_pipelined)
      verified nothing, so verified uploads of many small files with bundling
      off silently skipped integrity checking.
    
      Fix a decoupled-verify false positive.  The inline source-hash
      accumulator is per-connection and keyed by size alone; it was safe only
      because the classic inline verify consumes it on the same connection
      right after the upload.  The decoupled post-transfer verify can run on a
      different worker than the uploader, where the accumulator may hold a
      different same-size file's hash - a spurious mismatch on a byte-correct
      file.  sftp_hpn_verify_transfer gains a trust_inline_src argument: the
      classic path passes 1, the decoupled parallel path passes 0 and re-reads
      the source.

commit 77263a1a87120557e4337a44278408377ee0432a
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Fri Jun 19 18:01:20 2026 -0400

    sftp: run HPNVerifyTransfer as a post-transfer phase, not inline
    
      Verify previously ran inline on the transfer path: range-split files at
      tracker finalize, whole-file uploads/downloads right after the transfer
      in execute_unit. That blocked the finalizing worker on a server read-back
      mid-transfer and parallelized poorly.
    
      Verify now runs as a post-transfer phase. Completed verified files park
      their tracker instead of verifying in place; whole-file transfers park a
      lightweight tracker carrying just the paths + direction. Once the transfer
      queue drains, sftp_parallel_wait submits one SFTP_OP_VERIFY work unit per
      parked file onto the same workqueue; the now-idle workers pull them and
      verify in parallel on their own connections, off the transfer path.
      wait() blocks until the verify units drain.

