commit 30c968a3c5984e6aedaa88921357935e0b36f840
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Fri Jun 19 10:09:18 2026 -0400

    sftp: verify range-split uploads per range from teed source hashes
    
      Range-split uploads under HPNVerifyTransfer previously fell back to a
      whole-file source re-read at finalize. Tee an XXH3 of each range's source
      bytes during the send and verify each range against the server's per-range
      sftp-hash-range dest hash, so a clean transfer never re-reads the source;
      only ranges that did not tee cleanly are re-read over their own (off, len).
    
      Replace the per-range hash-range cap (SSHBUF_SIZE_MAX) with a file-relative
      total-work guard: reject a request whose EOF-clamped ranges sum past twice
      the file size. A legitimate verify or chunked resume tiles the file exactly
      (sum equals file size), so this admits any multi-GiB range while bounding a
      crafted overlapping request to reading the file at most twice.
    
      Two correctness guards on the teed hashes:
    
      - Store a teed hash only when the unit still covers the slot's original
        (off, len). The highwater-resume requeue advances the offset, shrinks the
        range, and resets attempt to 0, so the prior attempt==0 guard let a
        remainder-only hash land in a full-range slot, giving a false verify
        failure on byte-exact data after any transient connection drop.
    
      - Bound the slot array by its allocation count (vslots_n), not the tracker's
        total. The endgame split grows total with sub-range pieces but never
        resizes the array, so the finalize loop walked off the end, sent garbage
        ranges, and failed verify on a perfect copy. The split range's slot stays
        unset and its full-range re-read already covers every piece.

