commit 619fe2b233f150e3894e76f9554e4a51bb9cdd4e
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Wed Jun 17 15:39:53 2026 -0400

    Fix bundle truncation when a connection dies mid-extraction
    
      A bundle upload abandoned on a dead connection could leave a file
      silently truncated, even though every file was eventually transferred.
    
      Cause: when a bundle's connection dies mid-stream, the client re-queues
      all its files (all-or-nothing) and they re-send on another connection.
      But the dead connection's server process keeps draining the tar bytes it
      already received and writing those files (opened O_TRUNC). If that lagging
      extraction reaches a file AFTER the re-send already wrote it correctly, the
      O_TRUNC + partial write clobbers the good copy. With HPNVerifyTransfer off
      nothing notices; the transfer reports success. Reproduced deterministically
      (a 96KB-of-108KB truncation) by killing a connection mid-bundle against a
      slowed extractor.
    
      Fix, two parts:
    
        Client (correctness): on a dead-connection bundle failure, close the
        transport write pipe to tear the connection down, then wait
        HPN_BUNDLE_DEATH_REQUEUE_WAIT (3s) before re-queuing. The server's drain
        is bounded by the bundle in-flight cap, so the pause guarantees the
        re-send lands AFTER it and is the sole final writer. Failure-path only.
        3s is an arbitrary value that should handle even pathological writes
        on distributed filesystems but we can't offer a guarantee. It may be
        possible to accelerate the time in which the writer notices the dead
        connection and self terminates.
    
        Server (optimization): when the client half-closes (POLLHUP/POLLRDHUP)
        with an upload bundle still open, abort the abandoned extraction instead
        of draining its whole buffer - fewer wasted writes, prompt cleanup, and
        more margin for the client pause. Gated on an in-flight upload bundle. .

commit ee293ef6e5b4ce6d61d0a6a02b15356910ff2eac
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Wed Jun 17 12:26:25 2026 -0400

    Dispatch a bundle container that arrives via the leftover path
    
      The producer-side bundling change left a fatal hole: a SFTP_OP_BUNDLE_*
      container could reach execute_unit, which fatal_f's on a container op,
      aborting the whole transfer.
    
      Path: a transient bundle failure (e.g. a connection dropped mid-bundle)
      re-queues the bundle's members as individual SFTP_OP_UPLOAD units, so the
      work queue now holds both containers and individuals. A worker that pops
      an individual unit and enters the accumulate loop can trypop a container;
      being off-op it is assigned as the loop's `leftover` and dispatched
      through worker_execute_single -> execute_unit -> fatal_f. So a single
      transient on a large transfer killed the entire job (observed: a 30k-file
      upload aborted at 15420/30000 when one worker's connection dropped).
    
      Route containers correctly at the single-file entry point: if the unit is
      a bundle container, hand it to worker_dispatch_bundle_container and
      return. This covers the leftover path and any future path into the
      single-file dispatch; the execute_unit fatal_f stays as the backstop.
      Accounting is unchanged - the container's queued_bytes was already
      subtracted when it was trypop'd.

commit b4dd8b5106938defeac63940d3869252d1dde589
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Wed Jun 17 11:07:01 2026 -0400

    Assemble bundles producer-side to end the startup grab race
    
      This is out of order and is an issue discovered during the work on
      optimizing the verifcation work. Baically, the model we had for
      having workers grab files for their bundles was not properly
      organized and led to workers grabbing everything they could in a
      mad rush. This could leave files that shoudl have been bundled
      essentialy orphaned. One worker would have dozens of files in the
      bundle and other workers would only have one or two.
    
      This was especially true for downloads as the metadata would arrive
      as one large mass of metadata all at once. Think of it like pigs at
      a trough wheer you dump in a bucket of slop and they all fight for
      whatever they can get. Imposing order on this helps prevent that and
      ensures that workers get full size bundles.
    
      This was discovered while validating HPNVerifyTransfer on bundled downloads.
      Bbecause a few files never bundled, an injected corruption could land in
      an un-bundled file and be caught by the whole-file verify instead of the
      bundle verify - so the download bundle-verify path could not be tested.
      Which is why this fix is landing in the middle of the verify-testing work.
    
      We move composition to the single producer (the submit thread). Eligible
      small files are grouped into a SFTP_OP_BUNDLE_* container before they
      reach the queue; a worker pops a whole bundle and ships it - no
      accumulation, no race. Grouping is deterministic and the FIFO lines
      workers up by construction. Bundles are sized by framed wire bytes
      (512-byte header + 512-aligned data), not raw payload, so tiny-file
      header overhead cannot balloon a bundle past the cap; an 8192-file
      backstop remains. The partial tail flushes at sftp_parallel_wait.
      Transient bundle failures still decompose and retry through the
      worker-side path, now the fallback.
    
      Result: every eligible small file bundles.

