      to sftp-parallel-worker.c, and extend the dev-only SFTP_FAULT_CORRUPT
      injector to sftp_upload_range so a corrupt range can be exercised.

commit 0709262a5a09474b106dd687286b98fbe5150f47
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Tue Jun 16 13:58:40 2026 -0400

    HPNVerifyTransfer: on-disk O_DIRECT read-back + inline source hash
    
      Make the opt-in integrity verification (HPNVerifyTransfer) both correct
      about what it checks and cheap enough to be worth using.
    
      Correctness: the target's hash is now a fsync + posix_fadvise(DONTNEED) +
      O_DIRECT read-back of what actually landed on disk, instead of a buffered
      read that only re-validates the page cache. "Work follows the data" - the
      host that wrote the target reads it back: the server on upload, the client
      on download. Both directions now get the on-disk guarantee.
    
      Cost: the source hash is computed inline as the data is read to send, so a
      verified upload no longer does a second full read of the source. When the
      inline hash is unavailable (resume, etc.) it falls back to the prior
      buffered re-read.
    
      The read-back logic lives in one place, sftp-hpn-readback.{c,h}, shared by
      both binaries; the server's hpn-check-file handler now calls it and its
      private copy is removed. O_DIRECT is portability-guarded with a buffered
      fallback where it is unavailable.
    
      Also propagate verify_transfer_enabled to worker connections (it was set on
      the main connection only), so the inline accumulator arms in parallel mode
      and resume-decision hashes request HPN_CHECK_FILE_STRICT; and add a missing
      prototype for sftp_hpn_verify_transfer in sftp.c (was an implicit
      declaration).
    
      Disabled (the default) leaves the hot path untouched.
    
      Test scaffolding (compile-gated behind -DHPN_FAULT_INJECTION): a new
      SFTP_FAULT_CORRUPT=<offset> injector flips one byte of the data sent while
      leaving the on-disk source clean, so a verified transfer's source hash
      holds and the target diverges - the red/green anchor for the read-back.

