commit 45676df5bab6b3477afd6cc52d959e98c5356f3d
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Sat Jun 13 22:48:20 2026 -0400

    sftp: reuse open remote handle across consecutive same-file ranges
    
      Add a warm-handle cache to sftp_upload_range so a worker streaming
      consecutive ranges of the same file keeps the remote SFTP handle open
      instead of closing and reopening at every range boundary. This removes
      the synchronized close/reopen stall that appeared as a periodic
      throughput dip each time the equal-sized writers finished together and
      all re-opened at once.
    
      The handle is closed on error, file change, cooperative yield, idle, or
      worker exit, so behavior is unchanged in every case except the common
      one (next range, same file), where it saves a close/open round trip.
      On a connection wedge the worker self-terminates and respawns cold,
      which is identical to baseline; the surviving workers keep their warm
      handles. parallel_worker_close_warm guards on sftp_conn_is_dead before
      issuing sftp_close, so a dead connection is never blocked on.
    
      New struct sftp_range_warm carries the cached handle; sftp_upload_range
      gains a trailing warm argument (NULL preserves the old behavior, as used
      by the verify path). Worker state holds the cache; HPN_NO_RANGE_WARM
      disables the optimization at runtime, mirroring HPN_NO_BATCH_PIPELINE.
      This is a dev env var that will be removed after full testing. Right now
      it looks like this will be wired in as the default without user
      overrides.
    
      Validated by a 10-run interleaved 100GB A/B on a lossy path: integrity
      byte-exact every run, never slower than baseline, range-boundary dips
      suppressed, and markedly better recovery under matched packet loss.

commit 5a44c76e3950e1e5da04ca9d6ba96de28a64593d
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Sat Jun 13 15:05:35 2026 -0400

    Parallel streams: scan-idle respawn deferral + born-dead stuck-range guard
    
      HPN_RESPAWN_SCAN_IDLE=1 (default off): defer fleet-restoring respawns
      while READY healthy workers already cover the queued demand, avoiding new
      connections into a penalty-counting server.  Gated on fleet progress
      (noprogress_consec_ticks==0) so it never defers during a stall - the
      unguarded version doubled the slow-run rate; guarded it matches scan-idle
      -off and beats the old baseline in bad weather.
    
      Born-dead stuck-range suppression (always on): when successive born-dead
      reaps land on the same range_offset, the range is stuck server-side (a
      fresh worker born-dies on it too), not the connection - suppress the
      fast-kill and wait, with the silence brake as backstop.  Eligibility
      broadened so a proven worker handed the stuck range also qualifies (the
      case scan-idle's deferral creates).  No spurious fires in testing; its
      trigger condition never arose to validate in anger.
    
      This might be turned into default on after more testing.

