commit 7e29e682396fbfaba13876ffd56ea2d150b8cb99
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Fri Jun 12 21:45:11 2026 -0400

    Tail redistribution (phase C): cooperative yield of lagging holders
    
      When the tail detector latches a confirmed episode and the env gate
      HPN_TAIL_REDISTRIBUTE=1 is set, ask the SLOWEST lagging holder to
      cooperatively yield: the range transfer loop (both directions) stops
      issuing new requests/writes, drains everything already in flight (the
      most expensive bytes on a slow stream - keep them), and returns through
      the existing highwater machinery with acked exactly at the yield line.
      worker_process_result reclassifies the non-success as voluntary: the
      untouched remainder requeues at the front of the queue with no retry
      charge, marked so dispatch gives a different worker first crack (one
      courtesy defer, cleared on use, so a lone worker cannot deadlock on its
      own yield).
    
      Never a kill: the slow worker drains, goes READY, and can re-earn trust
      through the rate-window evidence rules.  A connection that dies during
      the wind-down reclassifies as an ordinary death and takes the validated
      death-resume path; watchdog coverage is unchanged.  One yield per
      episode, only when the holder's own projected remaining tail exceeds
      TAIL_PROJECT_SEC, and never against a worker without window evidence.
      Default off; with the gate off the hot path costs one relaxed atomic
      load per loop iteration.
    
      Interleaved tests with 50 on 50 off show that the median time was identical
      but that th ebig win was in the tails transformed:
      p95 110.0s -> 52.2s, max 173.3s -> 70.0s, run variance cut 2.8x.
    
      Detector-confirmed troubled runs averaged 102.4s off vs 52.6s on.
      The gate-off disasters (106-173s) were single sick workers grinding the endgame at
      14-35MB/s with episodes detected but action forbidden; gate-on
      equivalents handed the remainder to a healthy worker at full rate.
      5/5 yields fired on genuinely degraded runs, 0 spurious yields in 45
      clean runs, hpnsftp CPU unchanged (61% vs 58% mean).

commit 6cb1cfc46245a8dc5fba3658a50de5c928c28782
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Fri Jun 12 16:31:41 2026 -0400

    Fault injection: receive-side throttle for download stragglers
    
      Add SFTP_FAULT_THROTTLE_RECV=<bytes>:<delay_ms>[:<max_conns>], the
      download-side mirror of SFTP_FAULT_THROTTLE: after <bytes> received,
      every further message receive on the connection sleeps <delay_ms>.
      Delaying the read backpressures the local ssh child's pipe, which
      closes the SSH channel window, which stalls the server's stream - from
      every client instrument's view this is indistinguishable from a slow
      server, with no server-side scaffolding required.  Same slot
      discipline as the send throttle (default 1 connection, atomic claim,
      never kills); hook is one FAULT-INJ-tagged call at the bottom of
      get_msg_extended(), a no-op unless built with -DHPN_FAULT_INJECTION.

commit cd500250c983f6704f970cd2de92ff0c7b9bc74d
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Fri Jun 12 15:45:21 2026 -0400

    Tail detector: per-worker rate-window predicate and persistence gate
    
      Replace the fleet-median lagging test with per-worker evidence: the
      reporter samples each BUSY worker's personal rate into a small ring
      (idle workers retain their last demonstrated samples), and the lagging
      baseline becomes the median of READY healthy workers' window medians -
      "would the available workers demonstrably do this faster?"  Workers
      with fewer than WORKER_RATE_MIN_SAMPLES contribute nothing on either
      side, so a respawn's warmup can neither accuse nor be accused.  Fixes
      the measured failures of the old predicate: self-referential baseline
      collapse as the busy set shrank (episode flapping), idle-worker
      deflation, and respawn-warmup false positives.
    
      Add a persistence gate (TAIL_CONFIRM_SEC, 8s wall clock): the arm
      condition must hold continuously before an episode latches.  Measured
      basis: on shared-bottleneck transfers contention reshuffles and
      post-respawn ramps look identical to a straggler for 2-5s and
      self-resolve; a genuine throttled straggler lagged 36s continuously.
      Episode start backdates to lag onset so reported durations stay true.
    
      This is just the instrumentation so we can make the next round of
      attempts to deal with endgame handling based on evidence instead of
      "well, this looks like it might work"

commit b3960ef928d783ef83bce04f195fbd0802eac5fd
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Fri Jun 12 14:03:40 2026 -0400

    Park cap-gated workers on a queue activity channel instead of spinning
    
      As mentioned in the last commit the existing writer-cap-gate
      was spinning rapidly consuming about 44k useless call per
      second. This was having an impact on measurements we need to
      design the endgame strategy and wasting CPU. The fix is adding
      a kick-sequence condition variable to the workqueue: a worker
      whose full pass finds only capped units parks until a writer
      slot frees (kicked at release), new work is submitted
      (kicked at submit), shutdown, or a 250ms backstop. Basically, the
      writer manager kicks the workers active and then it draws a unit.
      The 250ms backstop covers events that the manager doesn't know
      about.
    
      Kicks are deliberately NOT raised by the queue's push primitives: the
      cap-gate's own requeues push there, and kicking from push created a
      wake/pass/requeue feedback storm before being removed.
    
      Backstop choice from a 3x3 sweep (100/250/500ms, big100 uploads):
      durations identical within noise across all values, denials scale
      deterministically (118k / 47k / 25k); 250ms takes the middle margins.
      Throughput unchanged by measurement. Fewer cycles burned means
      a happier host.

commit 151a9f5a327b40f88194da5911e11e30fd671dfd
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Fri Jun 12 13:14:36 2026 -0400

    Add worker availability intent and the tail trend detector (telemetry)
    
      Workers now publish availability at their voluntary transitions: READY
      (blocking in pop - purposefully idle, healthy), CAPPED (cycling the
      per-file writer-cap gate - available for other files), BUSY (holding a
      unit, size published for projections). Consumers read it with health;
      it never overrides the watchdog.
    
      On top of it, phase B of the tail-elimination design: the reporter
      samples fleet aggregate rate into a 25-tick ring and logs would-arm
      EPISODES (sustained decline or a projected long solo-tail, with idle
      capacity and a lagging holder) under HPN_BUNDLE_TIMING. Telemetry
      only - nothing is actuated.
    
      The first run showed tat the writer-cap gate (to ensure we don't cause
      contention writing to files) was consuming about 44k calls per second
      or about 6 million over a 100GB run. This is possibly throwing off our
      measurments and tainting the methodology. This will be fixed in the
      next commit before moving on.

commit 52c5b4452a37d8c525517f515071dbf103059ee4
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
