commit 2fd59a542f9e1e2ff0456b5ba1bdac82a1c2636f
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Fri Jun 12 11:56:09 2026 -0400

    Resume failed range units from their acked highwater; guard range exits
    
      This is part of a set of changes to rethink how we do endgame work.
      The initial method, automatically split the last unit across multiple
      workers no matter what caused timing issues that actually led to throughtput
      degradation, This turned out to be only visible when moving single files
      that were roughly N workers * Block (2GB default) in size. In other
      situations the degradation was hidden by other factors. We're now trying
      to be smarter about it.
    
      A range unit that fails or is reaped now requeues as only its unlanded
      remainder: upload/download_range report the contiguous-acknowledged
      highwater (in-order ack drain head for uploads; outstanding-request
      floor with failure clamps for downloads), and the requeue advances the
      unit past it. Worker death costs the in-flight window (~1% of a 10GB
      file) instead of the whole range - the dominant source of user-visible
      tail collapse. Progress resets the retry budget: only fruitless
      attempts count. Prior to this the entire workunit/block would be
      started over form the beginning. It seemed good enough/easy at the time.
    
      The fault-injection campaign validating this surfaced two other
      bugs, fixed here. Both range transfer loops could exit early and
      silently finalize a short range as success (upload: an unlogged local
      read failure; download: request-window collapse) - data loss. Both
      paths now assert their completion invariant at exit: a range that did
      not move every byte fails and retries. And the fault rig itself killed
      connections by closing their fds at the client layer (production never
      closes there), freeing descriptor numbers the kernel recycled into
      other workers' open files; the reaper's later by-number close then hit
      innocent fds. The rig now marks the connection dead like a real EPIPE.

commit 1f5d00ee201c1a3cdfd83f632a57325c5d254b04
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Thu Jun 11 20:58:36 2026 -0400

    Disable the endgame range split by default; add maturity gate
    
      Preserved state of the always-split approach at the point I stepped
      back from it (2026-06-11). Set HPN_ENDGAME_SPLIT=1 to re-enable for
      evaluation; the maturity gate (at least one completed range on the
      tracker before arming) is included and active when enabled.
    
      Issues causing re-evaluation
      - Large files: throughput benefit inside noise.
      - Files with fewer ranges than workers: every endgame condition is
        true at t=0, so the split armed at transfer start; the writer cap
        then released all pieces as one synchronized burst when the equal
        originals finished together, inflating path RTT 101->312ms
        (self-induced queueing) and dragging the tail. The maturity gate
        fixes t=0 but cap-requeue pops re-arm late on small files.
      - RTT-scaled grant pacing was tried and reverted: it made arming
        universal and could not be validated.
      - The rescue scenario (wedged worker holding the tail) is already
        covered by the watchdog's straggler reap and requeue.

commit 6aaadc098d3aa7755844fd0bb081d2baa3488678
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
