commit ed7de5725f561bd369d9db73637d1f089542121e
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Wed Jun 10 11:10:16 2026 -0400

    Split the final queued range at the endgame so idle workers drain it
    
      When the worker that pops the last queued unit leaves the queue empty
      (walker done), it shrinks the held byte-range to its first piece and
      submits the remainder as sibling range units, instead of one worker
      grinding the final range solo. N = the per-file writer cap (-w),
      one-shot per transfer, upload and download ranges both; whole-file and
      bundle units are never split. This resolves an issue where a final
      worker might be slow or wedged, which we saw a lot. By doing this we
      make more effective use of existing workers and minimize the amount
      of time draining the last workunit.
    
      Also publish walker DONE from sftp_parallel_wait and demote it on the
      next submit: previously only directory walks ever set DONE, so direct
      puts/gets never armed the endgame machinery (this split and the
      straggler reaper)

commit 36af911a12b6d2eb5535f72446170574ba3565d3
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Tue Jun 9 20:33:41 2026 -0400

    Add -w flag to set the per-file concurrent range-writer cap
    
      The cap on how many range-split workers may write one file at once was a
      compile-time constant (4). Make it a runtime option so it can be tuned per
      transfer without a rebuild.
    
      -w N sets the cap to N in [1, 10] (default 4, the measured throughput knee);
      the effective cap is min(-w, -j). Floor/default/max constants move to
      sftp-parallel.h, the value flows through sftp_parallel_config into each
      range tracker's writer_cap, and out-of-range values are rejected at parse
      time. No flag uses the default of 4. This value was derived through testing.

commit 21b838b4d83c27c071e91d11a963ccde0b657731
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Tue Jun 9 16:41:22 2026 -0400

      Cap concurrent range-writers per inode (default 4 - testing only)
    
      Range-split sends -j workers writing one file's single inode at once;
      buffered writes there serialize on the per-inode lock This can cause
      contention and slow performance. testing indicates that 8 workers is
      bad but 4 workers is good. So the goal is to allow us to have more than 4
      workers but limit each file being written to no more than four writers
      at a time.
    
      Approach: each file's range_tracker gets active_writers/writer_cap. A
      worker acquires a slot in the dispatch gate; if the file is at cap it
      requeues the unit and looks for other work (bounded spin: a 2ms sleep
      after a full pass of only-capped units). The slot is released in
      worker_process_result, the single choke point hit once per executed
      unit on success, retry-requeue, and give-up. The batch loop's leftover
      dispatch is gated the same way so a range unit diverted there stays
      balanced. Whole-file/bundle units have no tracker and are never gated;
      the tracker's finalize invariants are untouched.
    
      Recovers single-file throughput toward single-stream (not to the
      multi-file ceiling - buffered single-inode cannot parallelize). Client
      side only, no server change.

