commit de283572015d8b4268992db0889e9875d16b3889
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Thu Jun 4 18:46:59 2026 -0400

      serverloop: peer-stall wait + 5-min brake on the download path
    
      Mirror clientloop's wait-not-kill for the server-side (download sender)
      TCP-health consumer: PEER_STALL is now waited out (the client's receive
      window is pinned - a transient stall that recovers; reaping just churns a
      reconnect into the same busy client) instead of reaping at the 10s floor,
      and PEER_STALL_BRAKE terminates (exit 82) only after the stall is sustained
      past PEER_STALL_BRAKE_SECS (5 min). Up- and download now behave the same.

commit 8bb7a40faf1f3cefe3229e7cb17b1e1a41c9d3e4
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Thu Jun 4 18:33:36 2026 -0400

    sftp-parallel: peer-stall brake + fleet abort; drop A/B scaffolding
    
      - Peer-stall no longer self-terminates; it waits, and the classifier
        emits a new brake verdict only after a stall sustained 5 min
        (PEER_STALL_BRAKE_SECS) - upload side reaps then (clientloop,
        sftp-hpn-congestion).
      - Fleet abort: stop the transfer only when no worker is alive and
        reconnects are failing - zero aggregate progress for the window, no
        worker heart-beating, >= 2*num_streams consecutive respawns that each
        produced no bytes, and work still pending.
      - Born-dead kill threshold now derived from path RTT (clamp rtt/10 to
        5..40s) instead of a fixed 5s.

commit 08d618e6ba1f08c9a218138f35300111f91ebdd7
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Thu Jun 4 10:55:55 2026 -0400

    sftp-parallel: episode-aware operator flares for degraded transfers
    
      Add reporter_flare(), called once per reporter slow-tick after dispatch.
      A "degraded episode" is any contiguous stretch where the orchestrator is
      backing off respawns (cooldown active) or accepting slow-but-working
      workers (born-slow gated off). Edge-triggered framing replaces per-event
      log spam:
    
        - rising edge: one notice ("backing off: destination saturated,
          pausing connections" or "accepting N slow worker(s)") - no countdown,
          since the cooldown level escalates/decays and isn't actionable
        - sustained: reminders on a multiplicative back-off cadence (60s, x2
          per reminder, capped at 600s) so a long episode doesn't spam;
          escalates to warning wording after 5 min, with the pending count
        - falling edge: a recovery notice
    
      Suppressed under -q / -b (quiet stays quiet; errors still surface via
      error()). Best-effort framing throughout - a degraded episode is the
      transfer adapting, not failing. No-op on a clean transfer.
    
      The born-slow block is restructured so the gated-off path records
      born_slow_accepting and a debug line - the live signal for whether the
      chunk-4 born-slow gating helps or hurts on a real path. The two cooldown
      logs (enter, early-end) drop to debug_ft since reporter_flare now carries
      the operator-facing version.
    
      Prior commit was a misfire and only captured some of this work.

commit 139a7d3eb217cdeb50455dbe7eb3d78a98126541
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Thu Jun 4 10:41:44 2026 -0400

    sftp-parallel: episode-aware operator flares for degraded transfers
    
      Add reporter_flare(), called once per reporter slow-tick after dispatch.
      A "degraded episode" is any contiguous stretch where the orchestrator is
      backing off respawns (cooldown active) or accepting slow-but-working
      workers (born-slow gated off). Edge-triggered framing replaces per-event
      log spam:
    
        - rising edge: one notice ("backing off: destination saturated,
          pausing connections" or "accepting N slow worker(s)") - no countdown,
          since the cooldown level escalates/decays and isn't actionable
        - sustained: reminders on a multiplicative back-off cadence (60s, x2
          per reminder, capped at 600s) so a long episode doesn't spam;
          escalates to warning wording after 5 min, with the pending count
        - falling edge: a recovery notice
    
      Suppressed under -q / -b (quiet stays quiet; errors still surface via
      error()). Best-effort framing throughout - a degraded episode is the
      transfer adapting, not failing. No-op on a clean transfer.
    
      The born-slow block is restructured so the gated-off path records
      born_slow_accepting and a debug line - the live signal for whether the
      chunk-4 born-slow gating helps or hurts on a real path. The two cooldown
      logs (enter, early-end) drop to debug_ft since reporter_flare now carries
      the operator-facing version.

commit 16b57a21df290d2f103e95a71a2fc14b2992d60c
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Thu Jun 4 10:29:34 2026 -0400

    sftp-parallel: unify born-slow back-off with the respawn cooldown
    
      Born-slow and peer-stall are two views of the same backend-pressure
      problem at different timescales, so born-slow now shares the chunk-3
      escalating/decaying cooldown instead of carrying its own budget.
    
      A born-slow kill is a respawn: it pushes respawn_epoch_count and trips
      the epoch ceiling, entering the escalating cooldown. So born-slow needs
      no separate counter. Delete BORN_SLOW_MAX_KILLS and born_slow_kills, and
      gate the kill two ways:
    
        1. Fleet-state: fire only when a healthy peer exists
           (tput_last_raw_max_kbps >= floor) - a true outlier. When the whole
           fleet is slow (no healthy reference = backend saturation) born-slow
           is suppressed; that case belongs to the cooldown/probe machinery,
           and a respawn would just churn while re-queuing the unit.
        2. Cooldown: suppressed while a respawn cooldown is active. Once the
           shared cooldown engages we accept slow-but-working workers
           (best-effort); decay-on-health restores born-slow when the path
           recovers.
    
      Behavior change: born-slow no longer fires in the all-workers-slow case
      it was originally designed for (e.g. -j2 both stuck) - intended, so we
      don't thrash respawns against a saturated backend.

