commit 5aa7a5ccc97cd3833d63089811f47b5cc19fc945
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Mon Jun 1 14:18:50 2026 -0400

      Deliver the precise wedge cause to the client on a download
    
      On a server-side wedge, instead of a bare disconnect, send an exit-status
      channel request carrying the HPN_EXIT_TCP_* code on the session channel,
      then channel_force_close. The client's clientloop already turns exit-status
      into its process exit code, so the orchestrator reaps the precise cause and
      can label/respawn accordingly -- matching the upload direction, where the
      client exits with the code directly.
    
      channel_force_close resets only the channel data buffer, not the packet
      output where the exit-status request sits, so the code survives the
      teardown; the main loop flushes it best-effort without blocking. A hard
      wedge may still prevent delivery, in which case the worker dies and is
      respawned with a generic label -- the guaranteed floor.

commit 314cac66a28df2250f4aea17ed72a1e59fa59cfa
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Mon Jun 1 13:13:14 2026 -0400

      Add server-side TCP_INFO wedge detection for parallel workers (step 5)
    
      The download-direction side of the work. On a download the server is
      the data sender, so it's the side that can see a wedge the client only
      sees bytes not arriving.
    
      A worker advertises itself to its server with a new global request,
      hpn-parallel-worker@hpnssh.org (same mechanism as stack-metrics@hpnssh.org,
      purely an opt-in flag. It carries no data). If the operator permits it
      (new HPNWorkersDie sshd_config option, default yes), serverloop arms the
      shared TCP-health monitor on the connection and polls it off a ppoll
      deadline, the same traffic-independent way clientloop does. On a confirmed
      WEDGE or PEER_STALL the server terminates the connection (ssh_packet_disconnect
      with a descriptive reason); the client's orchestrator then respawns the
      worker. PATH_DEGRADED is logged only. note: Need to update the metrics
      option to use the same ppoll method. can we recycle the code?
    
      The classifier (sftp_hpn_classify) is shared, so both ends use identical
      logic; notsent>0 scopes it to whichever side is sending, so the server
      fires on downloads and stays quiet on uploads with no extra logic.
    
      HPNWorkersDie only ever affects connections that identify as parallel
      workers; ordinary sessions are never touched. Documented in
      hpnsshd_config.5 and the sshd_config sample

commit 585271f0c9e94869725c406d1eac109c8c61589a
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Mon Jun 1 12:07:37 2026 -0400

      Add the TCP_INFO wedge classifier and poll cadence (step 4)
    
      The monitor now watches the worker connection on a ppoll deadline
      (~1/sec, traffic-independent like ServerAlive) and acts on it.
    
      sftp_hpn_classify keys on active network distress, not absence of
      throughput. delivery_rate and the app_limited bit are recomputed only
      per delivered ACK (net/ipv4/tcp_rate.c), so they go stale during a
      no-ACK stall and can't tell a wedge from the app merely pausing (e.g.
      hashing during verification). The live, read-time signals are used
      instead:
    
      WEDGE: data queued (notsent>0) and, on kernels >= 6.7, RTO time actively
      accruing (tcpi_total_rto_time includes the in-progress episode).
      On < 6.7 (no RTO accounting) the fallback is a sustained collapsed cwnd
      corroborated by  retransmits in the window, so a slow/small-cwnd link with
      no loss is not mistaken for a wedge. Die.
    
      PEER_STALL: rwnd_limited climbing while cwnd is healthy ergo the peer
      isn't draining. Die.
    
      PATH_DEGRADED: steady retransmits while still moving. Logged only,
      don't kill yet.
    
      EXTENDED-only (6.7 kernels+) acts.
      BASIC polls and logs so its data can be evaluated later.
      notsent>0 scopes detection to the sending side (uploads); the download/server
      side comes later. Conservative thresholds, 10s sustain,
      LAN paths skipped. Per-field availability gating keeps a single
      distributed binary correct across kernel versions.
    
      Unit tests drive synthetic sample sequences through every verdict and
      guard: primary and < 6.7 fallback wedges, peer-stall, path-degraded,
      the idle-but-healthy veto, the slow-link guard, LAN skip, download,
      BASIC, and transient recovery

commit 9adc6beece4b05762343c04480e918a7585f4ed5
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Mon Jun 1 10:39:09 2026 -0400

      Add worker self-termination exit codes and reap-time death classification
    
      Groundwork for letting a parallel-streams worker bow out on its own when
      its transport goes bad. No decision logic yet -- this just lays down the
      exit-code vocabulary and the orchestrator side that reads it.
    
      hpn-exit-codes.h reserves the 80-89 block for transport self-termination
      (wedge, path-degraded, peer-stall) with an HPN_EXIT_IS_TCP() test. It's
      its own small header rather than part of sftp-client.h: clientloop has no
      business pulling in the whole SFTP client API for one constant, and the
      tree already keeps exit codes by concern anyway (srclimit.h, sftp-client.h).
    
      The reap path now looks at the wait status it used to discard, plus
      w->doomed, and logs how each worker actually died -- we killed it, it
      self-terminated, ssh error, clean exit, or a crash. Diagnostic only;
      respawn behavior is unchanged. The orchestrator also sets
      HPN_PARALLEL_WORKER in the worker's environment.
    
      When that variable is set, clientloop arms the TCP-health monitor on the
      connection (an initial TCP_INFO probe). Regular ssh sessions are left
      alone, and there's no periodic poll or wedge decision yet.
    
      sftp-hpn-congestion.o moves into libssh so the ssh binary can link the
      monitor; tcpi-portable.o was already there.
    
      Nothing emits the new codes yet, so the classifier only labels the deaths
      we already see. The poll cadence and the real wedge detection come next.

