commit cbac87872ab1c79fab1e01d29199deb8b84792c6
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Sun May 31 21:44:31 2026 -0400

    sftp-hpn-congestion: add TCP_INFO health-monitor polling primitive
    
    Introduce a per-worker TCP-health self-monitor for the parallel-streams
    path, in two layers:
    
      tcpi-portable.{c,h}      portable getsockopt(TCP_INFO) accessor.  Reads
                               the kernel struct only through named fields
                               against the local header, so the cross-platform
                               wscale / offset / privilege differences (Linux,
                               FreeBSD, NetBSD, OpenBSD) never bite.  Exposes a
                               normalised struct tcpi_portable plus a per-field
                               availability bitmask.  Extension/Tier-2 reads are
                               gated on the length getsockopt writes back -- not
                               on compile-time LINUX_VERSION_CODE -- so a single
                               distributed binary is correct across whatever
                               kernel it runs on (build kernel != run kernel is
                               the norm for packaged binaries).  Maps fields up
                               to the 6.2 rcv_wnd and 6.7 RTO trio.
    
      sftp-hpn-congestion.{c,h}  probe + poll + availability state machine
                               (UNPROBED -> UNSUPPORTED/PENDING/BASIC/EXTENDED).
                               Polling primitive only; no decisions yet.  The
                               file header documents the interplay with the
                               time-based watchdog: the watchdog answers "is
                               work happening?", this answers "is the network
                               the reason it isn't?" -- complementary, this
                               enriches the watchdog rather than replacing it.
    
    OpenBSD support is gated on HAVE_STRUCT_TCP_INFO_TCPI_SND_CWND, which
    configure.ac does not define yet, so OpenBSD currently falls to the
    unsupported path by design until that check is added.
    
    Will be building a test application next and then integration. The
    test application will be in regress.

