commit d2037bebe14a8e8afa688b0b03dae0c38850d001
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Sun May 31 14:41:51 2026 -0400

    sftp-hpn-tar: replace libarchive with in-tree USTAR codec
    
      libarchive's read_cb model is synchronous, but our SFTP wire is
      pipelined.  The impedance mismatch forced a queue + orphan-drain on
      the client download path (2026-05-31 bundle-DL bug) and pinned the
      server's whole bundle in RAM as a per-handle accumulator (~64 MiB ×
      workers worst case).  We used ~5% of libarchive's capability; the
      other 95% (pax/gnu/cpio/zip/iso, sparse, hardlinks, ACL/xattr,
      compression, encryption) was unused attack surface.
    
      Add sftp-hpn-tar.{c,h}: POSIX 1003.1-1988 USTAR with prefix-field
      path support (≤255 chars), header checksum read-verified and
      written, two-block zero EOA, regular files only.  Writer and
      parser are mirror state machines, no internal buffering beyond
      the 512-byte header scratch.
    
      Both ends switched:
    
        client download — streaming parser fed inline from SFTP DATA
        replies.  No queue.  Orphan-drain still present (small, kept for
        correctness) but no longer the source of complexity.
    
        client upload — writer drives a pack loop that produces tar
        bytes; bundle_ul_send_write ships them with the same adaptive
        in-flight cap the libarchive callback used to gate.
    
        server upload — bundle_write feeds the parser inline; entry
        callbacks open / write / close output files as bytes arrive.
        No accumulator.
    
        server download — bundle_fetch queues paths into the writer
        + finish(); bundle_read drives pack_next on demand.  No
        pre-packed in-RAM buffer.
    
      Server memory becomes O(1) per worker regardless of bundle size.
      Wire format unchanged; mixed old-libarchive / new-codec endpoints
      interop fine (USTAR-on-wire compliance preserved).

commit b3236c2cc6fd7d4091ff31f7dbdb58d4b346b04d
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Sun May 31 13:25:54 2026 -0400

     sftp-hpn-client: drain orphan in-flight READs before bundle close
    
      libarchive returns ARCHIVE_EOF as soon as it parses the tar
      end-of-archive marker (1 KiB of zero blocks), typically tens to
      hundreds of chunks before the server-side accumulator's true byte
      EOF.  The streaming receive loop, fed by the adaptive read-ahead
      cap and the memory-bounded queue, normally has many READs in flight
      at that moment.
    
      Without draining them they arrive on the wire AFTER our
      SSH_FXP_CLOSE STATUS and interleave with the NEXT bundle's READ
      replies, surfacing as "ID mismatch (X != Y)" sftp_conn_die calls
      and a worker abort.  Reproduced 2026-05-31 on juliet many-small
      download j=2: every bundle after the first failed with mismatch
      gaps of ~127 IDs.
    
      Add bundle_dl_stream_drain_inflight() — reads and discards
      (chunks_sent - chunks_received) replies from the wire before
      archive_read_close + SSH_FXP_CLOSE.  Skipped if stream.error is
      already set (conn likely dead; further get_msg would hang or fail).
    
      No server-side change; protocol-level fix on the client only.

commit 405c8b1aa9a2b77b45ed86e0c821636deebba097
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Sun May 31 13:01:00 2026 -0400

    sftp-hpn-client: stream bundle download with bounded queue
    
      The bundle download path previously buffered the entire server-side
      accumulator (up to HPN_BUNDLE_PER_CAP_DEFAULT = 64 MiB per bundle)
      in client memory before handing it to libarchive.  At -j 16 this put
      worst-case aggregate footprint at ~1 GiB just for in-flight bundle
      bytes.
    
      Replace receive-then-extract with a streaming pipeline:
    
        * bundle_dl_read_cb feeds libarchive from a FIFO chunk queue.
        * The receive loop fires READs and appends DATA replies to the
          queue tail; libarchive pops from the head and parses tar entries
          in-place, freeing each chunk as it is consumed.
        * Fire is bounded by both the adaptive read-ahead cap and a hard
          memory ceiling on (queued + in-flight) bytes.  When the cap
          would be exceeded the wire idles until libarchive drains —
          natural backpressure to slow extraction (e.g. Lustre metadata
          contention) without buffering arbitrary bytes.
    
      The memory ceiling is exposed as HPN_BUNDLE_DL_QUEUE_MAX_BYTES so
      we can sweep values during validation.  Default 32 MiB; accepts
      K/M/G suffix.  Promote to ssh_config once a preferred value emerges.
    
      Other changes folded in:
    
        * BUNDLE_DL_MAX_INFLIGHT lowered from 4096 to 1024 and demoted
          from throughput knob to safety bound.  The queue cap is now the
          primary tuning knob.
        * Per-request ring replaced with three counters.  SFTP IDs are
          issued sequentially within the receive loop, so the N-th
          outstanding reply's expected ID is first_read_id + chunks_received.
          Drops per-bundle tracking overhead from ~48 KiB to ~16 bytes.
        * Adaptive controller integration (rdahead_cap, rdahead_account,
          Part B backpressure timing on get_msg) preserved unchanged.
    
      Brings the download path to functional parity with the upload-side
      pipelined write_cb per the upload/download parity rule.

commit 2b7cfec7a32b03693a87e6fcbecea4fe02657e94
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Sun May 31 12:12:47 2026 -0400

    Revert bundle-truncation-hunt diagnostic instrumentation
    
      The 2026-05-31 truncation hunt produced a series of diagnostic
      instrumentation commits.  The hunt established that the truncations
      were not bundle code bugs (server-side accumulator integrity confirmed
      MATCH on every bundle, wire transport confirmed honest) but artifacts
      of harness timeouts killing workers mid-bundle.  The real issue is the
      synchronous bundle download path producing low throughput on high-RTT
      paths.
    
      This revert removes the diagnostic instrumentation in one commit while
      preserving the actual bug fix (f897d1e4e) and download parity.
    
      Reverts: f27b6b5761, 81047264c8, a6529574c1, 4e450b682

