commit f897d1e4ed43f56c055232f77c88ce77744e1b7a
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Sun May 31 08:32:54 2026 -0400

    sftp-hpn-server: fail bundle on mid-archive errors in pack_one
    
      bundle_fetch_pack_one previously returned 0/-1 and the caller
      (void)-discarded the result.  Per-file failures (open / fstat /
      non-regular skip) and mid-entry failures (libarchive header / data
      write errors, premature EOF on the source file) were lumped together
      and silently continued.
    
      For non-libarchive errors the pattern was correct ("one bad file
      doesn't kill the whole bundle"), but for mid-entry failures it left
      the libarchive writer mid-stream with a partially-written entry.
      Subsequent archive_write_header calls auto-padded the previous entry
      or shifted the archive into FATAL state; in either case the on-the-
      wire tar could ship structurally invalid.  Most visibly: the
      `n == 0 break` path treated a premature read() EOF as success
      despite having committed to writing sb.st_size bytes through
      archive_write_header, producing a short entry libarchive's read
      side rejects as "Truncated input file (needed N bytes, only 0
      available)" -- the failure mode observed in the 2026-05-31 br008
      many-small download validation campaign.
    
      Two-tier return code:
        0    entry packed successfully
       -1    pre-header metadata error (open / fstat / non-regular); caller
             safely skips this entry and continues with other paths
       -2    mid-entry failure (header / data write / source shrink);
             libarchive writer is mid-stream and cannot be safely
             resumed.  Caller abandons the bundle.
    
      Matches the upload-side bundle_pack_one error handling pattern in
      sftp-hpn-client.c.

