commit 7b97a9f89d390afcfafd2a63e9d106f81e69d929
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Sat May 30 20:23:28 2026 -0400

     sftp-hpn-client: reap connection on persistent floor degradation
    
      Parts A+B+C handle the common cases — bundle path adaptive cap,
      multiplicative decrease on STATUS timeout, time-based recovery probe
      at floor.  What remains is the case Part C's bounded oscillation
      cannot escape: a connection whose TCP state is permanently degraded
      (cwnd collapsed, RTO inflated, accumulated retransmits) where every
      probe immediately re-triggers backpressure and shrinks back to floor.
    
      Add persistent-degradation tracking on the rdahead controller.  Each
      backpressure event that lands cur==floor increments a counter and
      stamps first-arrival time; either threshold trips a reap signal:
    
        SFTP_HPN_RDAHEAD_REAP_BP_COUNT          5 events at floor
        SFTP_HPN_RDAHEAD_REAP_TIME_AT_FLOOR_SEC 60s at floor
    
      Reaping sets hpn->dead=1, feeding the existing orchestrator respawn
      machinery (cooldowns, total_respawns, BORN_SLOW budgets, session-wide
      gates).  Part D adds a trigger, not a parallel respawn path — thrash
      protection stays session-wide per the design discussion at
      reporter_dispatch_respawns (sftp-parallel.c).
    
      A fresh TCP session gets fresh kernel state, which cures TCP-wedge-
      class failures.  Path-wide degradation (Lustre OST contention,
      network congestion) re-triggers on the new connection; the session-
      wide cooldown bounds the resulting churn.
    
      State is cleared whenever cur leaves floor — Part C's force-probe or
      natural window-completion growth — so a later bad patch is accounted
      independently.

commit cad6dc1e18e2b7655b1ef5ad348e1281947b8705
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Sat May 30 20:16:14 2026 -0400

    sftp-hpn-client: time-based recovery probe at floor
    
      After Part B's backpressure signal shrinks the controller to floor
      with settled=0, the existing logic waits for a full window of `cur`
      acks before doubling.  At floor=64 on a slow path that takes tens
      of seconds.
    
      Add a time-based probe: when at floor with settled=0 and the current
      window hasn't filled in RDAHEAD_PROBE_INTERVAL_SEC (5s), force a
      doubling without waiting for a full window's worth of evidence.  If
      the path is still bad, Part B fires again and shrinks back to floor;
      bounded oscillation cycle of ~15s versus indefinite floor-stuck
      behaviour.  Restricted to cur==floor so probes never accelerate
      growth above the BDP knee on healthy paths.
    
      The constant is half Part B's threshold (10s): faster than Part B's
      reaction biases recovery toward higher depth, which is the right
      direction when path conditions ease.

commit e09140b375f76a5cbb5e4b21d9df8370b3e85569
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Sat May 30 20:09:54 2026 -0400

    sftp-parallel: per-worker respawn-timing fields + design comment
    
      Add first_reconnect_ns / last_reconnect_ns to struct sftp_worker
      (observability only — no gating decision is made on them) and surface
      both as first_reconnect_ms / last_reconnect_ms columns in the per-
      worker stats CSV.  Populated under w->mu at the same site as
      reconnect_count++.
    
      Existing respawn gates remain session-wide.  The 2026-05-30 Part B
      validation campaign (60 iters across br008 mixed-tree / br008 many-
      small / juliet many-small) did not produce a case where the session-
      wide pause wrongly froze healthy workers because of one bad one, so
      per-worker thrash gating is not added today.  When/if it becomes
      needed, the design discussion is captured in a comment block at
      reporter_dispatch_respawns — a time-windowed sliding detector
      mirroring RESPAWN_STABILITY_SEC's semantics, not a lifetime cap.
    
      The new fields exist so post-mortem forensics on the stats CSV can
      answer "which worker respawned and when" — the question we wanted
      answered while debugging the Phase 2b br008 wedges — without
      introducing a second gating system.

commit 420d5b55e745056811684a3475b2752595979053
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Sat May 30 18:42:43 2026 -0400

    sftp-hpn-client: add backpressure signal for wedge recovery
    
      Part A wired the bundle path into the adaptive read-ahead controller,
      but the controller is grow-only -- it probes upward, settles at the
      BDP knee, and has no mechanism to react when path conditions degrade
      after it settles.  The 2026-05-30 campaign captured 22 per-file
      Pattern 2 stalls where do_upload_body's get_msg blocked on a STATUS
      that wouldn't come back because the controller's settled depth was
      too high for the now-degraded path.
    
      Add sftp_hpn_rdahead_backpressure_signal(): multiplicative decrease
      (cur /= 2, clamped to floor), clear settled so re-probing resumes,
      discard the throughput baseline.  Analogue of TCP's RTO-triggered
      cwnd halving -- the SFTP-layer controller is doing application-level
      congestion control because it can't see TCP_INFO from across the
      hpnssh-subprocess boundary.
    
      Instrument three STATUS-read sites to time the read and fire the
      signal when it blocks longer than SFTP_HPN_RDAHEAD_BP_THRESHOLD_SEC
      (10s): do_upload_body, sftp_upload_range, bundle_drain_n.  10s is
      above legitimate Lustre OST slow-write latencies (3-8s) but well
      under the wedge durations observed in the campaign (all > 90s).
    
      Conn-side wrapper sftp_conn_rdahead_backpressure_signal() matches the
      existing watchdog/rdahead-cap wrapper pattern so HPN extension code
      that sees struct sftp_conn as opaque can fire the signal too.

commit 0ce9e4b02ab71e7ae15ced7e0cf252ca16e87932
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Sat May 30 11:32:54 2026 -0400

    sftp-hpn-client: wire bundle path into adaptive read-ahead controller
    
      The bundle upload path used a fixed BUNDLE_MAX_INFLIGHT=4096 cap on
      outstanding SSH2_FXP_WRITEs.  At the default 128 KiB libarchive block
      this allowed ~512 MiB inflight before the defensive drain engaged --
      far past the kernel pipe + TCP send buffer on low/medium-BDP paths.
      Result: writev to the hpnssh pipe and the subsequent STATUS read in
      bundle_drain_n both block for minutes under back-pressure (captured
      17x in the 2026-05-30 merge-validation campaign).
    
      Replace the fixed cap with sftp_hpn_rdahead_cap(), the existing
      adaptive controller already used by the per-file upload path.  Feed it
      real bytes-per-ack via a per-context wsizes ring so its throughput
      sampler measures the right thing.  Add sftp_conn_rdahead_cap /
      _account wrappers in sftp-client.c so the bundle code can stay opaque
      to struct sftp_conn (matches the existing watchdog-pause pattern).
    
      The controller probes from RDAHEAD_FLOOR (64) upward and settles at
      the BDP knee, so depth is small on LAN and large on HPC fat pipes.
      BUNDLE_MAX_INFLIGHT remains as the controller's upper bound and as
      the fallback when HPN_RDAHEAD=fixed disables adaptation.

