commit 46f35c936d52616985163249bf80a6b800e7cb64
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Fri May 29 14:42:04 2026 -0400

    sftp: report actual wire bytes; fix prefix-resume sparse-skip mis-fire
    
      Add a per-connection bytes_wired_payload counter incremented at every
      SSH2_FXP_WRITE send and SSH2_FXP_DATA receive, aggregated by the parallel
      orchestrator and surfaced in the end-of-transfer reporter line. The
      previous bytes_total_aggregate counts full work-unit sizes and includes
      data that chunked-resume verified-and-skipped, so its "throughput" was
      work rate, not wire rate. New format: "X transferred in Ys (Z MiB/s)"
      with a "; Y skipped via resume" beat only when nonzero — fresh transfers
      look unchanged.
    
      Building the metric surfaced a real bug: the verified-resume hash check
      on a partial remote file was passing flags=0, so the server's sparse-skip
      optimisation fired (length == st_size on the remote, even though the
      client was asking about a prefix of its longer local file) and returned
      HPN_HASH_FULLY_ALLOCATED_SENTINEL. The client compared the sentinel
      against its real prefix XXH3, saw a mismatch, and restarted the upload
      from scratch — wasting bytes already correctly on the peer. Both
      prefix-resume sites (upload and download) now pass HPN_CHECK_FILE_STRICT
      to force a real hash compare.

commit f258c598da144ca8889c490eebc60ec60ef8edab
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Fri May 29 13:45:51 2026 -0400

    sftp: replace hash-extension grace timer with server heartbeats
    
      Under parallel-worker contention on a single device, the grace formula
      in sftp_hpn_grace_for_hash() (1 GB/s assumed disk read + 30 s headroom)
      underestimated server-side hash time on big files, and the watchdog's
      born-dead fast-kill fired mid-hash. Workers died, their hpnssh transports
      closed, and get_msg() reported "transport receive failed." This is
      a nearly complete rethink of commit 71d4f28e watchdog restrictor.
    
      Replace the prediction with observation. Server emits a tiny "still
      working" reply every 5 s from inside the hash loop of
      process_extended_hpn_check_file (hpn-check-file) and process_hpn_hash_range
      (sftp-hash-range), using a reserved sentinel in the result field. Client
      loops over replies; each heartbeat refreshes the watchdog pause for
      another HPN_HEARTBEAT_REFRESH_SEC (30 s).

commit d15961fc61e4640e5c244a06dcf4f9701840bb19
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Fri May 29 10:49:39 2026 -0400

    sftp: sparse-skip optimization for hpn-check-file via sentinel
    
      Server short-circuits the read+hash and returns
      HPN_HASH_FULLY_ALLOCATED_SENTINEL (0xDEADBEEFCAFEBABE) when a client
      asks about a whole file (length == st_size) that's fully allocated
      (st_blocks*512 >= 95% of st_size). Client recognises the sentinel
      and treats the file as identical without computing the local hash
      either. Bilateral I/O + CPU savings; multi-file resumes where most
      files completed before the interrupt get a two-orders-of-magnitude
      wall-time reduction. This can be overridden by the user with the
      HPNVerifyTransfer option. Anyway, the idea is that this read+hash skip
      will allow the system to skip over files that are *likely* to be the
      same so it can focus on files that are *likely* to be damaged because
      of an interrupted transfer. It's not fool proof as it doesn't guard
      against files that may have changed between invocations but don't change
      the file size.
    
      Wire format: hpn-check-file request gains a uint32 flags field after
      length. Bit 0 = HPN_CHECK_FILE_STRICT — client sets it when
      HPNVerifyTransfer is enabled (user wants maximum verification, no
      trust shortcuts). sftp_verify_transfer (post-transfer integrity
      check) always passes STRICT.
    
      HPNVerifyTransfer state propagated onto conn->hpn via
      sftp_conn_set_verify_transfer at sftp_init. Resume-decision call
      sites (sftp_upload / sftp_download size-match branches) read it via
      sftp_conn_verify_transfer_enabled and pass the flag.

commit 71d4f28e0d525ad53841b66d91395c8122c0aad8
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Fri May 29 10:15:45 2026 -0400

    sftp: 50 GiB cap on chunked-resume; watchdog pause primitive
    
      Cap chunked-resume at CHUNK_HASH_MAX_FILE_SIZE (50 GiB).  Above this
      the cost-benefit math (f > BW/D) breaks down on moderate network
      speeds — hash eats most of the savings.  Falls through to existing
      whole-file gate.  Transitional defense; the planned sparse-skip
      optimization eliminates the case this cap protects against (fully-
      allocated huge files), so the cap should come out when sparse-skip
      lands. This is here in case we need to roll bock.
    
      Watchdog pause primitive: a worker can declare a window of legitimate
      non-byte-transfer work (verify-hash phase, fsync, bundle work, etc.).
      The orchestrator's inactivity heuristics suppress for that worker
      until the deadline expires.  SSH-child-gone still fires regardless.
      Fixes a pre-existing bug where the watchdog killed workers mid-hash
      on large files in the size-match resume branch (10 GiB hash ~25 s vs.
      born-dead threshold 5 s) — exposed when sftp_precreate's FSETSTAT fix
      started routing interrupted range-split resumes through that path.
      This is a generic function that we can use if we run across other
      situations where keeping the watchdogs at bay makes sense.

commit c55f210133a76afb611d138d05b451951c1a9ea3
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Thu May 28 19:27:14 2026 -0400

    sftp: chunked verified resume via sftp-hash-range@hpnssh.org
    
      New SFTP extension batching per-range XXH3 hash queries so verified
      resume re-transfers only mismatched chunks instead of truncating +
      re-uploading the whole file on size-match-content-mismatch.
    
      Wire format: (path, num_ranges, [(off, len)...]) -> [hash...].
      All-or-nothing error semantics; client falls back to hpn-check-file
      whole-file hash then to full re-transfer; server hash failure
      emits a user-visible logit_f.
    
      Server handler in sftp-hpn-server.c (process_hpn_hash_range);
      sftp-server.c gains only a dispatch wrapper, table entry, and
      advert.  Client helpers in sftp-hpn-client.c (xxhash_local_range,
      hash_remote_ranges, try_chunked_resume_{upload,download}).
      Decision flow in sftp_upload / sftp_download declines and falls
      through cleanly when the server lacks the extension, so this is
      backward-compatible against pre-19.0 servers.
    
      Also fixes sftp_precreate to set the logical EOF via FSETSTAT after
      open (process_open silently drops ATTR_SIZE; process_fsetstat
      honors it via ftruncate).  Without this an interrupted range-split
      upload leaves the remote at the highwater of pwrites rather than
      full size, so resume routes through prefix-resume instead of the
      size-match path the chunked code targets.  Documented intent of
      sftp_precreate matches the new behavior.

