commit 7e96bfa23ad4a5322272a69c3471e26db3b49b40
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Wed May 20 18:00:27 2026 -0400

    sftp-parallel: remove adaptive worker scaling
    
    Benchmarks showed adaptive scaling never wins against fixed -j N
    Scale-down never fired in any test. Scale-up only helped when -j
    was set too low, and even then a higher fixed -j was strictly
    better with 2-16× lower variance.
    
    So remove the adaptive machinery, keep the throughput-outlier
    detector (watchdog_sample_-throughput) and sync-stall observer
    as standalone fault detectors.
    
    Removed:
      - adaptive_scaling config field and -o AdaptiveScaling= parsing
      - reporter_thread scale-up/scale-down block
      - scale_up_thread, scale_ceiling, scale_bps*, scale_check_*,
        scale_cooldown_ticks, pending_scaleups, scale_shallow_streak
      - SCALE_* constants
    
    Kept:
      - watchdog (HEALTHY/STALLED/DEAD classification)
      - Respawn machinery (cooldowns, epoch ceiling)
      - Born-dead fast-kill (BORN_DEAD_KILL_SEC=5)
      - watchdog_sample_throughput (outlier detection of dribbling workers)
      - watchdog_check_sync_stall (observation-only writeback-stall metric)
      - Chunk-count fix: RANGE_CHUNK_MULTIPLIER × num_streams
    
    Net: ~540 lines removed. Fixed-pool -j N baseline preserved
    (5-iter benchmarks match pre-removal results within run-to-run noise).
    
    Full adapative scaliong implementation preserved on branch
    feature-adaptive-scaling-v1 to test in different environments
    where the trade-offs may differ.

commit 08d2ba9ff0027642fb332b76294e47329a4ee8d1
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Wed May 20 15:59:19 2026 -0400

    sftp-parallel: pipeline batch puts, fast-kill born-dead workers
    
      - Defer sftp_parallel_wait in batch mode: consecutive  lines no
        longer block on each other's tail, so workers stay loaded across files.
      Hooks added for doing this in ineractive mode. That will make that more
      of a job queue. This will be an argument - not the default.
    
    Base range-split chunk count on -j N (× RANGE_CHUNK_MULTIPLIER=4)
        instead of scale_ceiling, which was always 24 regardless of -j.
    When we were dealing with slow workers it would have to move a
    lot of data (Size/N). This makes those chunks smaller but as a
    multiple of the number of workers. It cannot go beow a minimum size.
    
    Some times workers just never get off the ground. This seems to be
    caused by the ppoll waiting for a response from the server that
    never arrives. I don't know why. What we do is reap those workers
    in 5 seconds and respawn them. This has led to much improved
    throughput as we getting active workers back into the pool
    faster.
    
    There are some extra elements in there that were used to support
    the benchmarking and tests. These will be removeed shortly.

