commit bdf4628314ad69d62350d398564bee5100dd3ace
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Tue May 19 17:32:49 2026 -0400

    sftp-parallel: detect wedged workers in all isolation conditions
    
      Three failure modes the previous watchdog missed:
    
        * A worker wedged on its first unit — last_completion_ns stays 0
          so the existing stall gates (gated on since_completion_ns > 0)
          never fire.  Add unit_start_ns and use
          effective_silence_ns = since_completion_ns > 0
              ? since_completion_ns : (now - unit_start_ns).
    
        * A worker dribbling at <1% of peer rate — bytes_total stays at 0
          until a unit completes, so the RTT-warmup gate never lifts and
          the tput-outlier path is suppressed forever.  Switch the gate to
          bytes_total + live_bytes (continuous progress, not completion-
          granular).
    
        * A solo worker holding the queue empty — peer-EMA outlier check
          has no peer to compare against, so a wedge falls through to the
          60 s isolation timeout.  Add ISOLATION_PROGRESS_STALL_SEC (15 s)
          gate: declare DEAD if a solo worker has held a unit >15 s AND
          its tput_ema_kbps is below tput_path_healthy_kbps, EMA warm.
    
      dispatch-diag debug lines (worker entry / popped / executed) retained
      for future investigation; quiet outside -vvv.

commit 7c0a22ea45a8d4d897926c6baf786c9add185714
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Tue May 19 16:06:26 2026 -0400

      sftp-parallel: fix range-split sizing, harden worker fault detection,
        add RTT-aware outlier warmup
    
      Bundles several fault-tolerance and correctness fixes for the parallel
      orchestrator that surfaced during br008/Lustre testing.
    
        * Range-split sizing (maybe_submit_upload, maybe_submit_download):
          the prior code set range_size = info.stripe_size (1 MiB on our
          Lustre setup) then clamped num_ranges to stripe_count.  With an
          N-stripe file far larger than stripe_size, the first N-1 workers
          got a single 1-MiB range each and the Nth worker got everything
          remaining (~99% of the file).  Result: parallelism was an illusion
          — one worker did all the real work, the rest finished in ms and
          sat idle.  Now: pick num_ranges first (one per OST, capped by
          max_ranges), then size each range to file_size/num_ranges rounded
          up to a stripe boundary.  Each worker writes a contiguous span
          that Lustre routes across all OSTs in its layout.  Same fix in
          the download path.
    
        * waitpid(WNOHANG|WNOWAIT) probe for ssh child liveness: the prior
          kill(w->ssh_pid, 0) && errno == ESRCH check missed the SIGKILL-
          then-zombie case because a zombie's pid is still in the process
          table — kill(zombie, 0) returns 0, not ESRCH.  This made the
          watchdog blind to externally-killed (or otherwise dying) workers
          until the worker thread happened to do I/O and notice the pipe
          EOF, which for an idle worker could be many minutes.  waitpid
          with WNOHANG|WNOWAIT correctly reports a zombie as "child has
          exited" without consuming it, so the reap loop's blocking waitpid
          still succeeds afterward.
    
        * App-layer RTT measurement on the control connection:
          sftp_parallel_set_path_rtt() and the matching path_rtt_us field
          on sftp_parallel.  Filled by sftp.c right after the orchestrator
          is started: three sftp_realpath(".") round-trips on the already-
          established control conn, median taken.  Cheap (~3 RTTs at startup)
          and includes the full stack delay each worker would see.
    
        * RTT-aware outlier warmup gate: a freshly-spawned (or respawned)
          worker is in TCP slow-start for ~log2(BDP / initial_cwnd) RTTs.
          Comparing its EMA to a path-max EMA from peers already at steady
          state condemns it instantly — the bug that caused tight respawn
          churn loops earlier today, where every replacement worker was
          immediately killed by the outlier path before it could ramp.  New
          gate: skip outlier escalation until the worker has transferred
          (peer_kbps × path_rtt × RAMP_RTTS) bytes, clamped to
          [RAMP_WARMUP_BYTES_MIN, RAMP_WARMUP_BYTES_MAX].  At 200 Mbps peer
          over 50 ms with RAMP_RTTS=20, that's ~25 MiB — typically <1 s of
          actual transfer at full speed, enough RTTs for cwnd to fill the
          pipe.  Falls back to the fixed-tick warmup if path_rtt_us == 0.
    
        * Adaptive-scaling guards against worker-death noise: when a worker
          is first marked DEAD by the watchdog, the throughput drop it
          causes would misfire sat_signal against the now-stale pre-death
          bps reference and ratchet scale_ceiling downward.  Clear
          scale_bps_at_last_up and impose a cooldown on the first DEAD
          transition.  Separately, gate scale-down evaluation on
          pending_rs == 0 — scale-up was already gated this way, scale-
          down was not, so a respawn-in-progress could falsely trigger
          further scale-downs while the worker pool was already short.
    
        * Debug surfacing: log when the in-flight gate actively suppresses
          idle_signal (queue empty but total_in_flight >= nw), so the test
          harness can verify the gate is firing.

commit bb9e9bc1697a588b6c151efb1adc4905d8dc602f
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Tue May 19 13:00:30 2026 -0400

      sftp-parallel: fix adaptive scaling idle_signal to account for in-flight work
    
      Scale-down idle_signal fired whenever queue depth was zero, regardless of
      whether workers had units actively in flight.  With all files popped but
      transfers in progress (common at end of a batch or during large-file range
      splits), this caused premature scale-down — workers were removed, remaining
      workers starved, and threads blocked in futex for the duration of the
      remaining transfers.
    
      Fix: replace qdepth < nw*UPLOAD_BATCH_SIZE with
      total_in_flight + qdepth < nw, computed in the existing per-worker mutex
      loop.  Scale-down now requires that the sum of queued and in-flight work
      units is genuinely less than the number of workers — i.e. there is real
      surplus capacity, not just an empty queue.
    
      Adaptive scaling remains opt-in via -o AdaptiveScaling=yes.

