commit a2d46b32aa2dcde85471b6f56cbe03aabe3f2eac
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Mon May 18 15:58:44 2026 -0400

      sftp-parallel: harden worker lifecycle + adaptive stall detection
    
      This is a long one so hang on.
    
      Multiple related improvements to the parallel-streams orchestrator,
      driven by real test failures on br008 at 50 ms RTT.
    
      Critical fix — SIGPIPE no longer kills the parent process:
      * ssh_signal(SIGPIPE, SIG_IGN) at the top of main(). Without this,
        a worker thread writing to its (SIGTERMed) ssh child's pipe
        delivered SIGPIPE to the whole sftp process, killing the control
        connection and the orchestrator as collateral. Race-prone but
        reproducible — transfers died after the first watchdog kill.
        Every other OpenSSH binary already ignores SIGPIPE; sftp was the
        outlier.
    
      Worker reaping — SIGKILL escalation:
      * Watchdog now records doom_ns (DOOM!) when sending SIGTERM and escalates
        to SIGKILL after 5 s if the worker hasn't set exited=1. Breaks
        the deadlock where SSH hangs in clean-disconnect on a broken
        socket, the worker thread is blocked on the frozen ssh stdout
        pipe, and the reap path (which already does belt-and-suspenders
        SIGKILL) never runs because it's gated on exited=1.
    
      Adaptive throughput-outlier stall detection:
      * Opt-in companion to the existing time-based watchdog. Catches
        cwnd-collapsed workers that still complete the occasional file
        at ~0.1 % of healthy throughput, never crossing the 60/120 s
        staleness threshold.
      * Per-worker EMA (alpha default 0.2, ~5-tick time constant) plus
        EMA-based max for the outlier threshold; raw max is reserved
        for the path-health gate so threshold doesn't lag on recovery.
      * 5-tick warmup grace per worker so newly-spawned/respawned
        workers aren't classified as outliers during TCP slow-start.
      * Env-var-driven config (SFTP_TPUT_HEALTHY_KBPS, SFTP_TPUT_FRACTION,
        SFTP_TPUT_CONSEC, SFTP_TPUT_EMA_ALPHA) for first-pass tuning;
        promote to flags once defaults settle.
    
      Respawn lifecycle — cooldown + owed-respawn tracking:
      * Replace the hard "abort after 2 * num_streams respawns" ceiling
        with an epoch system: epoch ceiling triggers a 30 s cooldown
        pause (healthy workers keep running) then resets the epoch.
      * After RESPAWN_MAX_COOLDOWNS exhausted cycles, fall back to a
        throughput-gated abort: if any worker is still healthy
        (raw max_kbps >= path_healthy threshold), grant an uncounted
        cooldown extension with a loud warning instead of aborting —
        designed for petabyte-scale long-lived transfers where
        intermittent churn shouldn't be fatal.
      * RESPAWN_STABILITY_SEC (5 min) timer resets the cooldown count
        if no new cooldowns have fired, so accumulated churn over hours
        doesn't compound into an unwarranted abort.
      * respawn_owed counter carries deferred respawns across cooldowns
        and pthread_create failures so the pool drains back up to
        num_streams once spawning resumes.
    
      Worker death — graceful failure paths instead of fatal():
      * New sftp_conn_die() / sftp_hpn_conn_die() helpers in
        sftp-client.c set conn->hpn->dead and let the caller propagate
        the failure normally.
      * Convert several fatal() calls (Unexpected ACK, Expected
        SSH2_FXP_STATUS, Can't find request) to die-gracefully so a
        single worker's protocol error doesn't abort the whole transfer.
      * Check get_msg_extended() return value in places that previously
        fatal'd on a short read.
    
      Diagnostics:
      * logit() the control ssh child PID at startup so post-mortem
        analysis can distinguish it from worker PIDs.
      * HPNSFTP_CONTROL_VERBOSE env var (1/2/3 -> -v/-vv/-vvv) injects
        verbose flags into the control ssh without bumping sftp's own
        log level. Mirrors HPNSSH_WORKER_VERBOSE for symmetry.
      * cfg_port_buf on struct sftp_parallel owns the cfg.port string,
        fixing a dangling-pointer bug where respawn argv inherited a
        pointer into a stack frame that had since been reused — the
        "Bad port 'put *'" symptom.

