commit b0b0411acc3beaba794f4a9d2de834995f1b7118
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Sat May 16 12:11:58 2026 -0400

    Exprimental. Try to deal with paths that get stuck in slow start
    or some other pathological condition that prevents autotune from
    working correctly in the face of loss.
    
    Thsi likely won't work or work very poorly.
    
    channels: add TcpRcvBufRescue to escape the small-window trap
    
      On a lossy path an SSH flow can get stuck in a pathologically
      small-window state: early loss collapses cwnd, the sender trickles
      data, the receive buffer never fills, autotune doesn't grow it,
      and the peer advertises a tiny window for the rest of the
      connection's life. Even after cwnd recovers, throughput is then
      capped at rwnd/RTT.
    
      Add an opt-in TcpRcvBufRescue option (both client and server) that,
      when enabled, makes channel_tcpwinsz() forcibly grow SO_RCVBUF when
      TCP_INFO shows the trap signature: tcpi_min_rtt > 5ms (skip LAN)
      and tcpi_total_retrans > 0 (path has been lossy). The buffer is
      doubled, capped at SSHBUF_SIZE_MAX and the kernel's rmem_max, with
      a 1-second cooldown.
    
      Setting SO_RCVBUF locks the socket out of kernel autotune
      (SOCK_RCVBUF_LOCK), so subsequent rescues act as a userspace
      autotune that can grow further if needed.
    
      Defaults to off; requires TcpRcvBufPoll (the default) to take
      effect, since it rides the existing dynamic-window machinery in
      channel_check_window().

