commit 225d7268faf9cc3374d64366aa3dd4c22118d9d4
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Fri May 8 18:32:59 2026 -0400

    sftp-parallel: byte-based scale-up trigger, speculative range splitting,
    opt-in autotuning (tentative — may be rolled back)
    
    Changes in this commit:
    
    1. Byte-based scale-up trigger.  The scale-up decision was previously
       qdepth >= num_workers * UPLOAD_BATCH_SIZE (a unit-count threshold).
       On workloads with large units (single huge file, range-split units)
       the queue never reaches that count even with gigabytes of pending
       work.  Replaced with queued_bytes >= num_workers *
       SCALE_UP_MIN_BYTES_PER_WORKER (64 MiB).  New queued_bytes atomic is
       updated at every submit/pop/retry site so the counter leads the
       queue state.
    
    2. Speculative range splitting (then narrowed).  Initially submitted
       large files split into up to MAX_WORKERS ranges regardless of FS
       type, so the scale-up trigger could fire on a single huge file.
       Empirical testing showed concurrent writes to one inode on a regular
       POSIX FS widen throughput variance dramatically (CV from 20% to 50%)
       without lifting the mean.  Reverted: range splitting now only fires
       when sftp_fs_info reports valid Lustre/GPFS stripe geometry.  On
       regular FS, files are submitted whole and the scaler relies on
       multi-file parallelism.
    
    3. Opt-in autotuning (sftp.c).  The orchestrator now starts only when
       the user explicitly passes -j N.  Default behaviour without -j is
       plain single-stream sftp (zero behavioural change vs upstream).
       With -j N, the value is the starting point for the adaptive scaler
       rather than a fixed worker count.
    
    4. sftp_parallel_submit_upload accepts a control conn so single-file
       puts route through maybe_submit_upload (matching the recursive
       walker path).
    
    5. live_counter increment in sftp_upload_range.  Range writes were not
       reporting incremental progress, so the scaler's bps measurement read
       0 between range completion events and misfired the saturation
       signal.  Now increments per successful ACK, matching
       sftp_upload_batch.
    
    6. retired_bytes accumulator.  When a worker exits (scale-down), its
       bytes_total was lost from snapshot_workers, causing
       aggregate_bytes_for_meter to decrease and bps to underflow on
       uint64.  Captured on reap so the aggregate stays monotonic.
    
    Why this may be rolled back.  Benchmark data on non-parallel
    filesystems shows the autotuning machinery is technically correct but
    provides only 4-8% mean improvement on 30-second test transfers while
    doubling throughput variance (CV from 20% to ~50%).  Most of the cost
    is ramp-up time — the scaler takes 10-15 seconds to add workers,
    which is a large fraction of short transfers.  Sustained gains are
    likely to require a) much longer transfers where ramp-up is amortized,
    or b) parallel filesystems where range splitting earns its keep.
    Until there is empirical evidence that autotuning improves the typical
    user experience, this work is being carried as tentative; if
    real-world workloads do not benefit, revert is the right call.

commit 30df89649bbc67de6acedb6ce78dddda1e51a204
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Fri May 8 16:05:58 2026 -0400

      sftp-parallel: Phase 3b byte-range parallelism for large file uploads
    
      Split large files (≥64 MiB) across parallel workers by byte range when
      -j N with N≥2. Each worker writes its assigned byte range using
      standard SFTP v3 offset I/O, with the remote file pre-created at the
      correct size so concurrent writers don't race on creation.
    
      Add the hpn-fs-info@hpnssh.org SFTP extension. The server reports
      filesystem type (via statfs magic numbers), Lustre/GPFS stripe geometry
      (via fork/exec of lfs getstripe), and optimal block size. The client
      uses stripe boundaries to align range splits on parallel filesystems;
      falls back to equal block-aligned splits when the extension is absent or
      the filesystem has no stripe information.
    
      Integrate Phase 3b with Phase 3a adaptive scaling via live_worker_count():
      range splits track the current live worker count rather than the
      configured -j N value, so the adaptive scaler acts as the unified
      governor for both worker count and split factor.
    
      Remove the adaptive scaler's floor at num_streams: scale-down signals
      now use nw > 1 rather than nw > p->cfg.num_streams. -j N is the
      starting point for the transfer; workers scale freely from 1 to
      SFTP_PARALLEL_MAX_WORKERS based on observed throughput.

commit 3e0973f3f09852a2e4e3a9c4c408a1b578cff812
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Fri May 8 12:20:57 2026 -0400

      sftp-parallel: Phase 3a adaptive worker scaling
    
      Add a reporter-driven adaptive worker scaling system to the parallel
      SFTP orchestrator.  The reporter thread evaluates two independent
      enforces a 10-second cooldown between consecutive scale events.
    
      Scale-up fires when the work queue is saturated (depth ≥ nw ×
      UPLOAD_BATCH_SIZE) and throughput has improved ≥15% since the last
      scale-up (SCALE_UP_MIN_GAIN=1.15).  The 15% threshold prevents adding
      workers at a bandwidth cap where each new connection adds protocol
      overhead without increasing total throughput.
    
      Two independent scale-down signals share the same 10-second cooldown
      and floor of the user-requested stream count (-j N):
    
        Idle: a worker's effective idle fraction (completed idle_ns plus
        any in-progress blocking pop, captured via pop_start_ns atomic)
        exceeds SCALE_DOWN_IDLE_FRAC=0.35, indicating underload.
    
        Saturation: throughput has dropped to less than 85% of the bps
        measured at the last scale-up (SCALE_DOWN_SAT_THRESHOLD=0.85),
        indicating the added worker is hurting rather than helping.
    
      The idle and saturation thresholds form a symmetric 15% dead-band with
      the scale-up gain threshold, preventing oscillation when throughput
      sits right at the margin.
    
      A scale ceiling ratchets down when a saturation scale-down fires,
      preventing the removed worker from immediately being re-added.  The
      ceiling resets when throughput exceeds SCALE_CEIL_RESET_GAIN=1.30 times
      the bps at which the ceiling was set, indicating conditions have
      genuinely changed.
    
      All three behaviours verified under netem:
        - Scale-up + idle scale-down: 2→5 workers at 100ms RTT, then
          5→2 during 120s idle post-transfer
        - Scale-up guard: blocked at 20 Mbit/s cap after 2→3 (throughput
          plateau, guard prevented 3→4)
        - Saturation scale-down: 2→3→4 then 4→3 when 4th worker dropped
          throughput below 85% threshold at 3 Mbit/s cap; ceiling set to 3
    
      Also fix a fd leak in spawn_worker_ssh: pipe() fds now have FD_CLOEXEC
      set immediately via FD_CLOSEONEXEC() so they are not inherited by
      subsequent fork-exec calls for additional scale-up workers.

commit 8f58c6cf57b236ed167c60379604be6ed0e1aff0
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Thu May 7 17:56:59 2026 -0400

    Phase 3c: pipeline small-file writes across the batch in sftp_upload_batch
    
    For files that fit in a single SSH_FXP_WRITE (size <= upload_buflen,
    default 128 KB), the previous implementation called do_upload_body
    sequentially per file, paying one write-ACK RTT per file. For a batch
    of N small files this cost N RTTs regardless of stream count.
    
    Replace Phase 3 with four sub-phases:
      3a  burst all small-file SSH_FXP_WRITE requests without waiting for ACKs
      3b  collect all STATUS replies in one pass (one RTT for all N writes)
      3c  apply preserve_flag / fsync_flag to successfully written small files
      3d  large files (> upload_buflen) continue through do_upload_body, which
          already pipelines multi-chunk writes internally — no regression there
    
    Adds write_id to struct batch_file to track each file's pending write
    request ID. Empty files (size == 0) skip the write; large files skip
    phases 3a-3c entirely.
    
    Benchmarked against juliet.psc.edu, 4 streams, 20 000 x 25 KB files:
      RTT    before       after     speedup
        0ms   71 MiB/s   100 MiB/s   1.4x
       10ms    7.8 MiB/s  62.5 MiB/s  8.0x
       25ms    3.5 MiB/s  38.4 MiB/s 11.1x
       50ms    1.6 MiB/s  23.1 MiB/s 14.1x
      100ms    0.8 MiB/s  13.0 MiB/s 17.1x
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 4.6 <noreply@anthropic.com>

