    This sends files in batches without necessaruly waiting for the
    remote to confirm that the file has been opened and written correctly.
    
    this code tracks the files in flight and matches success messages with
    entries in the batch and removes them as those message come in. This
    should help when transferring lots of small files across a network
    with an RTT high enough to impose stalls when waiting for the
    confirmation message. This does seems to smooth out the transfer rate.
    
    This isn't the optimal solution though. Better would be for the sftp
    process to, effectively, do a tar pipe but that is a lto more work and
    impacts the protocol.

