commit 1b7475b320eaad1656980e4e540c215bf1f85691
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Thu Apr 9 18:09:22 2026 -0400

    Dynamic send window with better queueing of send buffer.
    
    Replaces the hardcoded 128KB output buffer limit in
    ssh_packet_not_very_much_data_to_write() with the limit from
    SO_SNDBUF (tcp_wmem on linux). This lets HPN-SSH keep the send
    buffer full. Before that it was limited to 128KB. That's not
    nearly as impactful as managing the receive buffer but it makes
    a difference.
    
    Also, now that we can keep the send buffer full we shoudl write
    more to it at once. In channel_output_poll_input_open() we were
    only enqueing 32KB at a time. Now it loops until the send buffer
    is empty, we hit backpressure, or thr remote window closes.
    This fille the pipeline faster and make more efficient use of
    our resources.
    
    Initial tests indicate that this is about 15-20% faster at RTTs
    where the send side buffer would have an impact (tested at 25, 50, 75ms...).
    This *may* end up increasing memory pressure in the kernel as we
    are pushing more data to the socket buffers. However, this should be
    limited by controls like net.core.wmem_max so we won't push things
    to exhaustion.

commit 3ce278375d9f0e649bd279a9122c4b5fc98966a3
Author: Chris Rapier <rapier@psc.edu>
Date:   Wed Apr 8 11:47:53 2026 -0400

    Useless NULL check on xmalloc in cipher-ctr-mt-providers.c in provider_ctx_new().
    
    xmalloc calls fatal() if it can't allocate the memory and will,
    therefore, never return NULL on failure. Everything dies before
    the function returns. So checking for NULL is useless. More of a
    correctness issue than anything else.

